d in action space, untrained uses cosine distance in hidden space. These are fundamentally different metrics — ratios across them need careful interpretation\n- Estimated acceptance probability: ~0.35 (needs reviewer champion)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the framework genuinely novel? Is R² exit collapse a new finding or an expected consequence of fine-tuning? How novel is the trained-vs-inherited distinction?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Only 10 references. Are important prior works missing? (mechanistic interp in multimodal models, probing in LLMs, instruction grounding in robotics, etc.)\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the 4 contributions actually 4 distinct contributions, or is this padding? Is the framework (C1) truly transferable? Is C3 (trained vs inherited) trivial? Is C4 (three-tier) justified with n=1?\n4. **Claim calibration**: Are claims well-calibrated to evidence? Does \"consistent across three tested architectures with caveats\" match 8/10 cells where one architecture is 50%?\n5. **Relevance to CoRL**: This is purely representational analysis with no behavioral/task performance connection. Is this sufficient for a robotics venue?\n6. **Positioning**: How does this compare to concurrent/prior work on VLA interpretability?\n\n## Output Format\nProduce a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Missing References/Comparisons**\n- **Scores**: Overall (1-10), Soundness (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Confidence (1-5)\n- **Decision recommendation**: Strong Accept / Accept / Weak Accept / Borderline / Weak Reject / Reject / Strong Reject\n","uuid":"ea471337-6eb4-418a-a58c-da379a0b7e58","session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus: **novelty and related work positioning**. \n\n## Venue Calibration\n- CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning), ~30% acceptance rate\n- OpenReview 1-10 scale: 10=top 5% seminal; 8=top 15% clear accept; 6=marginally above threshold; 5=marginally below; 4=not good enough; 3=clear reject; 1=trivial/wrong\n- CoRL values: significance/novelty of results, objectively established findings, relevance to robot learning\n- Limitations section honesty is valued\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Token-Level Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Analysis\"\n\nThe paper presents a systematic experimental framework for token-level analysis of VLA models, comprising R² probing, layer-wise attention decomposition, cross-instruction classification, counterfactual sensitivity analysis, and RSA. Applied across 3 VLA architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) and 4 LIBERO task suites.\n\n**4 claimed contributions**:\n1. A systematic token-level analysis framework (R² probing, attention decomposition, CF perturbation, RSA) applicable to any decoder-only VLA, validated on 3×4 matrix (10 cells)\n2. R² exit collapse — selective loss of action-relevant info in exit layers — is a consistent, training-induced signature across 3 architectures (8/10 cells, p=0.002 Wilcoxon), with 2 exceptions attributable to specific training configurations\n3. Distinction between training-induced effects (R² collapse, CF sensitivity) and base-model-dependent effects (L1 head counts)\n4. Suggestive three-tier routing gradient (VLM base → OXE-pretrained → task-finetuned), requiring further investigation\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)**: Very brief, 3 paragraphs:\n- VLA architectures: OpenVLA [2], TracVLA [3], NORA [4]\n- Mechanistic interpretability: probing [8], attention analysis [9], RSA [10]\n- Instruction following in robotics: Grant et al. [5], Haon et al. [6]\n- Only 10 references total in the paper\n\n**Key results**:\n- R² exit collapse confirmed in 8/10 cells. Two weak: OpenVLA Spatial (Δ=0.037, and CF ratio INVERTED at 0.81×!) and OpenVLA Long/OFT (Δ=0.004)\n- L1 attention heads are base-model property, not training-induced\n- CF ratios hugely asymmetric: Tier-1 baselines give 7-10× ratios, Tier-2 (OXE) gives only 0.81-2× — paper acknowledges this and reports separately\n- Three-tier gradient rests on n=1 Tier-2 datapoint (OXE)\n- Exit collapse rate analysis: continuous metric, Wilcoxon p=0.002, r=0.81\n- All analysis is representational, NOT behavioral — no connection to downstream task performance\n\n**Discussion & Limitations (Section 6)**:\nThe paper explicitly acknowledges 6 limitations:\n1. 2/10 weak cells qualify \"architecture-independent\" to \"consistent with caveats\"\n2. Three-tier gradient has only n=1 Tier-2\n3. NORA only 2/4 suites\n4. CF ratios not comparable across architectures\n5. All representational, no behavioral validation\n6. OFT confound for OpenVLA Long\n\n**Appendix A**: Vision encoder geometry — RSA shows inverted-U but CKA doesn't replicate. Explicitly downgraded to \"observation.\"\n\n## Experiment Design Context\nKey issues from the full experiment design:\n- OpenVLA has only 50% cell pass rate (2/4 confirmed), while TracVLA is 100% (4/4) and NORA 100% (2/2). Reviewers can question: \"if the most studied architecture only passes 50%, how can you claim architecture-independence?\"\n- The Spatial CF inversion (0.81×) means training REDUCED instruction sensitivity for that cell — this directly challenges the narrative\n- Three-tier gradient: Only 1 Tier-2 datapoint (openvla-7b OXE). Paper downgrades to \"suggestive observation\" but still lists it as Contribution 4\n- CF metric inconsistency: trained uses Cohen's d in action space, untrained uses cosine distance in hidden space. These are fundamentally different metrics — ratios across them need careful interpretation\n- Estimated acceptance probability: ~0.35 (needs reviewer champion)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the framework genuinely novel? Is R² exit collapse a new finding or an expected consequence of fine-tuning? How novel is the trained-vs-inherited distinction?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Only 10 references. Are important prior works missing? (mechanistic interp in multimodal models, probing in LLMs, instruction grounding in robotics, etc.)\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the 4 contributions actually 4 distinct contributions, or is this padding? Is the framework (C1) truly transferable? Is C3 (trained vs inherited) trivial? Is C4 (three-tier) justified with n=1?\n4. **Claim calibration**: Are claims well-calibrated to evidence? Does \"consistent across three tested architectures with caveats\" match 8/10 cells where one architecture is 50%?\n5. **Relevance to CoRL**: This is purely representational analysis with no behavioral/task performance connection. Is this sufficient for a robotics venue?\n6. **Positioning**: How does this compare to concurrent/prior work on VLA interpretability?\n\n## Output Format\nProduce a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Missing References/Comparisons**\n- **Scores**: Overall (1-10), Soundness (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Confidence (1-5)\n- **Decision recommendation**: Strong Accept / Accept / Weak Accept / Borderline / Weak Reject / Reject / Strong Reject\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fkf1rVNGL1iMs2op5SNsMN","session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3","uuid":"dafe1f69-3c11-4af3-ba20-645da611a62a","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:22:52.700Z"}
