{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E613QwHcKbjCocKPJiAbAR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tWhat does fine-tuning change inside a Vision-Language-Action model (VLA)?\n5\tVLAs fine-tune large language models on robot demonstrations to produce actions from visual observations and language instructions~\\cite{brohan2023_rt2,kim2024_openvla}, but the decoder-only causal mask prevents image tokens from attending to instructions~\\cite{wang2025seeing,pei2025rethinking}.\n6\tInstruction information must reach action-generating positions through learned attention patterns, yet how fine-tuning reshapes these patterns remains uncharacterized.\n7\t\n8\tRecent behavioral studies reveal that VLAs rely disproportionately on visual input~\\cite{grant2026}, with language instructions largely ignored by the action pathway~\\cite{haon2025}.\n9\tThese findings suggest internal specialization but do not explain \\emph{how} instruction information reaches the action-generating readout token, \\emph{what} attention strategy fine-tuning learns compared to language pretraining alone, or \\emph{whether} training creates new representational structure or reconfigures inherited geometry.\n10\tExisting analyses have not combined systematic per-layer probing with factorial trained/pretrained/untrained controls, leaving the representational mechanisms of VLA fine-tuning opaque.\n11\t\n12\tWe address this gap by decomposing OpenVLA-7B~\\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024}, a VLA chaining a frozen DinoSigLIP vision backbone~\\cite{zhai2023_siglip,oquab2024_dinov2}, an MLP projector, and Llama-2 7B~\\cite{touvron2023_llama2}.\n13\tWe extract representations from each of 32 LLM layers at both image-token and readout-token positions, and from external baselines (DINOv2~\\cite{oquab2024_dinov2}, CLIP~\\cite{radford2021_clip}).\n14\tWe compare three LLM conditions (VLA-trained, pretrained Llama-2 base, and randomly initialized) crossed with instruction presence, and apply four complementary analyses: cross-instruction probes for token-level specialization, L1 attention pattern analysis, RSA~\\cite{kriegeskorte2008} for geometric alignment, and MLP probes~\\cite{alain2017} for functional decodability.\n15\t\n16\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n17\t\\centering\n18\t\\includegraphics[width=0.88\\linewidth,height=2in,keepaspectratio]{figures/fig_teaser.pdf}\n19\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n20\t\\caption{\\textbf{VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention to entry-exit layers.}\n21\t(a)~Architecture overview.\n22\t(b)~Instruction-dominant heads per layer (L1 attention $>0.5$). Pretrained Llama-2 peaks at mid-depth; VLA-trained OpenVLA concentrates at L1 and L32. Untrained: zero heads at all layers.}\n23\t\\label{fig:teaser}\n24\t\\end{figure}\n25\t\n26\tThree findings emerge.\n27\t\\emph{First}, VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention from mid-depth to entry-exit layers, supported by seven complementary analyses including dose-response and counterfactual ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:learned_routing}).\n28\t\\emph{Second}, a $2{\\times}2$ trained-vs-untrained comparison across two causal-decoder VLAs---OpenVLA (Llama-2) and TraceVLA (Phi-3-V), which start from dramatically different untrained profiles---reveals a consistent reorganization pattern: both reach similar mid-layer action-decodability peaks after training ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.003$), with training effects concentrated at the readout token rather than image geometry (\\S\\ref{sec:nonmonotonic}).\n29\tIf routing were purely architecture-specific, different backbones should not arrive at the same functional profile; the observed consistency across two architectures is compatible with VLA training discovering a common organization constrained by the causal-decoder design.\n30\tArchitecture-expected token specialization (instruction-blind image tokens, perfect readout classification from L1) is verified as a framework sanity check (\\S\\ref{sec:token_specialization}).\n31\t\n32\tOur contributions are: (i)~evidence that VLA fine-tuning redistributes instruction attention from mid-depth to entry-exit layers, consistent across two causal-decoder VLAs, with L1 as the primary hub and redistribution strength modulated by task instruction-discriminability; (ii)~a consistent reorganization pattern across two causal-decoder VLAs with dramatically different starting points, consistent with training discovering a common functional organization, with effects concentrated at the readout token rather than image geometry; and (iii)~a systematic framework for token-level representational analysis of VLAs with factorial controls, including token specialization as a methodology validation.\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XCAbMmQAGqoFNBwh8Xxaok","session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3","uuid":"eb656cf9-90ff-4c83-8c41-e3b7d2419195","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:22:54.483Z"}
