{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TBiQFx7hYduayETfGe5fHE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\paragraph{Use of Generative AI.}\n2\tGenerative AI tools (Claude, GPT-4) assisted with code implementation and manuscript editing; all scientific content was produced by the authors.\n3\t\n4\t\\appendix\n5\t\n6\t\\section{Supplementary Material}\n7\t\\label{sec:appendix}\n8\t\n9\tUnless noted otherwise, all analyses use OpenVLA-7B (\\texttt{openvla/openvla-7b} on HuggingFace) evaluated on LIBERO-Goal (10 tasks $\\times$ 50 demonstrations, $N = 63{,}228$ observations after excluding terminal frames).\n10\tModel conditions, representation extraction, metric definitions, and probe architectures follow \\S\\ref{sec:setup}--\\S\\ref{sec:probes}.\n11\tCross-benchmark experiments (\\S\\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}) use LIBERO-Spatial with the same protocol (10 tasks $\\times$ 50 demonstrations).\n12\tAll experiments ran on 2$\\times$ NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPUs (96\\,GB each); random seed is 42 unless stated otherwise.\n13\t\n14\t\\subsection{CKA Analysis Method}\n15\t\\label{sec:cka_appendix}\n16\t\n17\tWe employ Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA)~\\cite{kornblith2019} alongside RSA as a complementary perspective.\n18\tWhile RSA captures local geometric alignment via rank-order preservation, CKA measures global representational similarity through kernel-based comparisons.\n19\t\n20\tWe compute both \\emph{linear CKA}, which uses a linear kernel and is sensitive to second-order statistics, and \\emph{RDM kernel CKA}, which operates on the same distance matrices as RSA but aggregates them differently.\n21\tUsing both variants tests whether conclusions about representation structure are robust to the choice of similarity metric.\n22\t\n23\tLinear CKA between high-dimensional representations (4{,}096-D for LLM layers) and the 7-D action space is subject to a known dimension mismatch: the linear kernel in the low-dimensional space may not capture the same structure as in the high-dimensional space.\n24\tRDM kernel CKA avoids this issue by operating on distance matrices of equal size regardless of the original feature dimensionality.\n25\t\n26\t\\subsection{Metric Sensitivity: RSA versus CKA}\n27\t\\label{sec:metric_sensitivity_appendix}\n28\t\n29\tLinear CKA~\\cite{kornblith2019} reverses the RSA ordering: untrained LLM ($\\text{CKA} = 0.089$) $>$ trained LLM ($0.083$) $>$ DinoSigLIP+Projector ($0.081$) $>$ DINOv2 ($0.066$), partially inverting the self-supervised advantage (Figure~\\ref{fig:metric_comparison}).\n30\tRDM kernel CKA partially resolves this discrepancy: trained ($0.260$) $\\approx$ Projector ($0.257$) $>$ untrained ($0.246$) $>$ DINOv2 ($0.241$).\n31\tThis divergence reflects the known sensitivity of representation similarity metrics to different geometric properties.\n32\t\n33\tAt the per-layer level, linear CKA between image\\_mean and the action space rises monotonically from L0 ($0.081$) to L32 ($0.098$), with no inverted-U pattern.\n34\tRSA captures rank-order geometric alignment; CKA captures global linear structure.\n35\tThe dissociation indicates that the geometric refinement in early layers operates on the relative arrangement of representations (affecting RSA) without altering the overall covariance structure (captured by CKA).\n36\t\n37\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n38\t\\centering\n39\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig6.pdf}\n40\t\\caption{Three similarity metrics across representation sources. \\textbf{(a)}~RSA ranks DINOv2 cls ($0.692$) $>$ Fused ($0.634$) $>$ Projector ($0.615$). \\textbf{(b)}~Linear CKA reverses the ordering: Untrained ($0.089$) $>$ Trained ($0.083$) $>$ Projector ($0.081$) $>$ DINOv2 ($0.066$). \\textbf{(c)}~RDM kernel CKA: Trained ($0.260$) $\\approx$ Projector ($0.257$) $>$ Untrained ($0.246$) $>$ DINOv2 ($0.241$). The metric divergence underscores that RSA and CKA capture different aspects of representational structure.}\n41\t\\label{fig:metric_comparison}\n42\t\\end{figure}\n43\t\n44\t\\subsection{Branch-Split Detailed Analysis}\n45\t\\label{sec:branch_split_appendix}\n46\t\n47\tTo disentangle the contributions of the two encoder streams within DinoSigLIP, we extract representations from the DINOv2 branch (1{,}024-D) and SigLIP branch (1{,}152-D) separately before fusion.\n48\tThe two branches yield near-identical RSA: DINOv2 branch $\\rho = 0.619$, SigLIP branch $\\rho = 0.621$.\n49\tFusion produces a representation ($\\rho = 0.634$) that exceeds either branch alone; concatenation captures complementary structure.\n50\tThe MLP projector then compresses this signal: DinoSigLIP+Projector achieves $\\rho = 0.615$, a drop of $\\Delta = 0.019$ from the fused 2{,}176-D input.\n51\t\n52\t\\subsection{RSA Across Representation Sources}\n53\t\\label{sec:rsa_bar_appendix}\n54\t\n55\tFigure~\\ref{fig:rsa_bar} shows RSA with the action space across all representation sources; the ordering matches Table~\\ref{tab:rsa_overview}.\n56\t\n57\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n58\t\\centering\n59\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig2.pdf}\n60\t\\caption{RSA with the action space ($\\rho$) across representation sources. Self-supervised DINOv2 \\texttt{cls\\_token} ($\\rho{=}0.692$) achieves the highest geometric alignment. CLIP ($0.557$) yields the weakest alignment among pretrained encoders. Error bars: 95\\% bootstrap CIs.}\n61\t\\label{fig:rsa_bar}\n62\t\\end{figure}\n63\t\n64\t\\subsection{Per-Layer RSA Profiles}\n65\t\\label{sec:rsa_perlayer_appendix}\n66\t\n67\tFigure~\\ref{fig:rsa_perlayer_curve} extends Figure~\\ref{fig:layer_curves} (main text) by adding readout and untrained profiles.\n68\t\n69\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n70\t\\centering\n71\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_rsa_perlayer_curve.pdf}\n72\t\\caption{Per-layer RSA ($\\rho$) for trained and untrained models at image\\_mean and readout (last\\_preaction) positions. The trained model shows an inverted-U peaking at L12 for both positions; the untrained model shows monotonic decline. After L12, readout and image\\_mean diverge: readout $\\rho$ declines gradually ($0.628 \\to 0.584$) while image\\_mean $\\rho$ drops sharply ($0.628 \\to 0.422$), consistent with task-specific conditioning at the readout position.}\n73\t\\label{fig:rsa_perlayer_curve}\n74\t\\end{figure}\n75\t\n76\t\\subsection{Per-Layer Action Decoding Across Training Stages}\n77\t\\label{sec:action_r2_threecond}\n78\t\n79\tFigure~\\ref{fig:action_r2_threecond} compares per-layer action probe $R^2$ across three training stages (untrained, Llama-2 base, VLA-trained) using 50 demonstrations per task, averaged over 3 seeds (mean $\\pm$ std).\n80\tAll three conditions share L0 representations ($R^2 = 0.645 \\pm 0.008$); subsequent differences thus arise from Transformer processing rather than input variation.\n81\tThe trained model peaks at L9 ($R^2 = 0.643 \\pm 0.014$), consistent with progressive specialization for action-relevant feature integration.\n82\tLlama-2 base shows a steady decline (L0: $0.645 \\to$ L32: $0.551$, std $0.003$--$0.035$), consistent with stable but unspecialized processing.\n83\tThe untrained model exhibits high cross-seed variance and deep-layer collapse, with negative $R^2$ and bimodal behavior across seeds at late layers; this instability indicates that randomly initialized Transformer layers produce destructive interference on action-relevant structure.\n84\tRandom weights at intermediate layers produce anti-correlated representations ($R^2 < 0$, L10--L11), consistent with untrained networks lacking structured computation.\n85\tThe three-condition gradient (trained $>$ Llama-2 base $>$ untrained) holds from L1 through L15, with a reversal at L32 where trained $R^2$ drops below the pretrained baseline.\n86\tThe qualitative pattern is consistent across all 3 seeds.\n87\tThree conditions show substantial agreement in layer-wise $R^2$ ordering (Kendall's $W = 0.72$, $\\chi^2 = 68.99$, $p < 0.001$).\n88\t\n89\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n90\t\\centering\n91\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_action_r2_threecond.pdf}\n92\t\\caption{Per-layer action probe $R^2$ (image\\_mean) across three training conditions: untrained VLA (random weights), Llama-2 base (pretrained, no VLA fine-tuning), and VLA-trained. All conditions share L0 ($0.645 \\pm 0.008$). Trained model peaks at L9; Llama-2 base declines steadily; untrained shows high cross-seed variance and deep-layer collapse. 50 demonstrations per task, 3 seeds (mean $\\pm$ std).}\n93\t\\label{fig:action_r2_threecond}\n94\t\\end{figure}\n95\t\n96\t\\subsection{Random-Subset Ablation Details}\n97\t\\label{sec:random_subset_ablation}\n98\t\n99\tTo evaluate whether the ablation effect in \\S\\ref{sec:learned_routing} is specific to instruction-dominant heads, we compare dominant-head ablation against 30 random subsets of 24/32 L1 heads (each subset drawn without replacement).\n100\tDominant-head ablation increases action $R^2$ by $+0.030$ (within-experiment baseline $0.524$), while random subsets yield a mean increase of $+0.071 \\pm 0.013$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:random_subset_ablation}).\n101\tThe dominant-head effect falls below all 30 random subsets ($z = -3.11$, percentile $= 0\\%$); heads with higher instruction-attention fraction carry more organized attention signals, consistent with graded rather than binary specialization.\n102\t\n103\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n104\t\\centering\n105\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_specificity_bootstrap_30seeds.pdf}\n106\t\\caption{$R^2$ increase from ablating 24/32 L1 heads. Gray bars: 30 random subsets; red line: dominant-24 heads ($+0.030$). Random subsets yield larger gains (mean $+0.071 \\pm 0.013$; $z = -3.11$, percentile $= 0\\%$), consistent with graded attention specialization across L1 heads.}\n107\t\\label{fig:random_subset_ablation}\n108\t\\end{figure}\n109\t\n110\t\\subsection{Action Trajectory Analysis Details}\n111\t\\label{sec:action_trajectory}\n112\t\n113\tTable~\\ref{tab:action_shift_perdim} reports per-dimension mean absolute shift for instruction-dominant (24 heads) and control (8 heads) L1 ablations.\n114\tBoth conditions produce negligible shifts on translational dimensions ($dx$, $dy$, $dz$ $< 0.01$) and small rotational shifts ($rx$, $ry$, $rz$ $< 0.04$).\n115\tThe gripper dimension absorbs the majority of the effect: instruction-head ablation shifts gripper predictions by $0.291$ (control: $0.141$), consistent with L1 attention influencing discrete open/close decisions.\n116\t\n117\tPer-task Cohen's $d$ values for instruction-head ablation are $0.43$, $0.69$, $0.70$, $0.70$, and $0.73$ (mean $0.65$), with all tasks significant ($p < 10^{-10}$).\n118\tToken-level KL divergence between original and ablated action distributions is $5.08$ for instruction heads and $1.69$ for control heads.\n119\t\n120\t\\paragraph{Trajectory-level aggregation.}\n121\tAggregating per-demo mean actions to the trajectory level reveals that token-level $d$ values are conservative: within-trajectory averaging reduces noise, yielding ${\\sim}4\\times$ larger effect sizes (mean token-to-trajectory ratio $0.245$).\n122\tInstruction-head ablation ($N = 50$: 5 tasks $\\times$ 10 demos) yields trajectory $d = 2.943$ (95\\% CI $[2.469, 3.707]$); bottom-24 non-instruction heads ($N = 30$) yield $d = 2.347$ (CI $[1.854, 3.221]$).\n123\tAll 14 conditions produce trajectory $d > 2.0$ with CI lower bounds $> 1.7$.\n124\tCounterfactual trajectory-level $d$ (10 LIBERO-Goal tasks, $N = 100$): wrong $3.798$ (CI $[3.194, 4.938]$), empty $3.438$ ($[2.921, 4.448]$), shuffled $2.595$ ($[2.028, 3.791]$), paraphrased $2.131$ ($[1.740, 2.819]$).\n125\tToken-level $d$ underestimates trajectory-level effects by ${\\times}3.1$--$4.5$, consistent with within-trajectory averaging reducing noise.\n126\tFine-grained ordering is not reliably distinguished at trajectory level ($N = 100$); token-level comparisons ($N {\\sim} 5{,}000$) provide higher resolution for ordering claims.\n127\t\n128\t\\paragraph{Ground-truth action error.}\n129\tAblation redirects model outputs ($d = 0.65$) but does not systematically increase error relative to expert demonstrations: original MSE $= 0.498$; top-24 instruction-head ablation MSE $= 0.497$ ($\\Delta = -0.001$, Wilcoxon $p = 0.52$, n.s.); bottom-24 non-instruction heads MSE $= 0.506$ ($\\Delta = +0.008$).\n130\tChanges concentrate on the gripper dimension; translation and rotation dimensions are negligibly affected.\n131\tPer-task breakdown shows all $|\\Delta\\text{MSE}| < 3\\%$ of baseline with all $p > 0.27$; the near-zero aggregate $\\Delta$ reflects uniformly small per-task effects rather than cancellation across tasks.\n132\tThis is consistent with the information-computation dissociation: instruction-dominant heads control instruction-specific direction selection rather than action precision.\n133\t\n134\tTable~\\ref{tab:perhead_bootstrap} reports per-head bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples).\n135\tIndividual control heads produce ${\\sim}1.9\\times$ stronger effects ($d = 0.051$) than instruction heads ($d = 0.027$, $p = 0.0018$), consistent across all 5 tasks.\n136\tThe 24 instruction heads nonetheless produce a larger aggregate perturbation ($d = 0.65$ vs.\\ $0.41$), consistent with collective coordination rather than individual strength.\n137\t\n138\t\\begin{table}[h]\n139\t\\centering\n140\t\\caption{Per-head bootstrap analysis of L1 ablation effects. Instruction heads are individually weaker but collectively stronger, consistent with coordinated attention redistribution.}\n141\t\\label{tab:perhead_bootstrap}\n142\t\\small\n143\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n144\t\\toprule\n145\tGroup & $N$ heads & Collective $d$ & Per-head $d$ [95\\% CI] \\\\\n146\t\\midrule\n147\tInstruction-dominant & 24 & 0.65 & 0.027 [0.026, 0.029] \\\\\n148\tControl & 8 & 0.41 & 0.051 [0.048, 0.055] \\\\\n149\t\\midrule\n150\tDifference & --- & --- & 0.024, $p = 0.0018$ \\\\\n151\t\\bottomrule\n152\t\\end{tabular}\n153\t\\end{table}\n154\t\n155\tInstruction-head ablation produces more uniform perturbation across action dimensions (CV${}=1.30$ vs.\\ control CV${}=2.74$, permutation $p = 0.016$), consistent with structured, language-conditioned computation rather than diffuse noise.\n156\t\n157\tTable~\\ref{tab:dose_response} and Figure~\\ref{fig:dose_response} report the dose-response gradient for instruction-head ablation at L1.\n158\tTrajectory divergence increases monotonically from $d = 0.482$ (4 heads) to $d = 0.639$ (32 heads), with steep rise through 12 heads (slope $\\Delta d / \\Delta 4 \\approx 0.026$), a plateau at 16--20 heads ($\\Delta d = 0.001$), and slow increase at 20--32.\n159\t\n160\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n161\t\\centering\n162\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}\n163\t\\caption{Dose-response gradient: trajectory divergence (Cohen's $d$) as a function of ablated instruction-dominant heads at L1. Error bars: 95\\% bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples). Dashed red: control ablation (8 non-instruction heads, $d = 0.41$). Steep rise through 12 heads, plateau at 16--20, diminishing returns beyond.}\n164\t\\label{fig:dose_response}\n165\t\\end{figure}\n166\t\n167\t\\begin{table}[h]\n168\t\\centering\n169\t\\caption{Dose-response data for L1 instruction-head ablation. Cohen's $d$ vs.\\ original action sequence; 95\\% bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples).}\n170\t\\label{tab:dose_response}\n171\t\\small\n172\t\\begin{tabular}{rcc}\n173\t\\toprule\n174\tHeads ablated & Cohen's $d$ & 95\\% CI \\\\\n175\t\\midrule\n176\t4 & 0.482 & [0.449, 0.511] \\\\\n177\t8 & 0.508 & [0.479, 0.541] \\\\\n178\t12 & 0.560 & [0.529, 0.593] \\\\\n179\t16 & 0.592 & [0.559, 0.625] \\\\\n180\t20 & 0.593 & [0.559, 0.627] \\\\\n181\t24 & 0.611 & [0.578, 0.645] \\\\\n182\t28 & 0.619 & [0.587, 0.654] \\\\\n183\t32 & 0.639 & [0.603, 0.671] \\\\\n184\t\\bottomrule\n185\t\\end{tabular}\n186\t\\end{table}\n187\t\n188\t\\begin{table}[h]\n189\t\\centering\n190\t\\caption{Counterfactual instruction analysis across all 10 LIBERO-Goal tasks. Jaccard: token overlap with original instruction; Cohen's $d$: mean across 10 tasks. Token novelty ($1 - \\text{Jaccard}$) largely predicts divergence ordering ($\\rho = 0.8$); the one violation (shuffled $>$ paraphrased) reflects positional sensitivity. L1 ablation included for comparison.}\n191\t\\label{tab:counterfactual}\n192\t\\small\n193\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n194\t\\toprule\n195\tCondition & Jaccard & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n196\t\\midrule\n197\tEmpty (no instruction) & 0.000 & 0.978 \\\\\n198\tWrong (different task) & 0.174 & 0.924 \\\\\n199\tShuffled (random order) & 1.000 & 0.763 \\\\\n200\tParaphrased & 0.533 & 0.707 \\\\\n201\t\\midrule\n202\tL1 ablation (24 heads) & --- & 0.650 \\\\\n203\t\\bottomrule\n204\t\\end{tabular}\n205\t\\end{table}\n206\t\n207\t\\begin{table}[h]\n208\t\\centering\n209\t\\caption{Mean absolute action shift per dimension under L1 head ablation. Instruction: 24 dominant heads; Control: 8 remaining heads. Gripper shift dominates both conditions.}\n210\t\\label{tab:action_shift_perdim}\n211\t\\small\n212\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n213\t\\toprule\n214\tCondition & $dx$ & $dy$ & $dz$ & $rx$ & $ry$ & $rz$ & Gripper \\\\\n215\t\\midrule\n216\tInstruction (24) & 0.002 & 0.005 & 0.008 & 0.021 & 0.031 & 0.034 & 0.291 \\\\\n217\tControl (8) & 0.000 & 0.001 & 0.002 & 0.008 & 0.014 & 0.018 & 0.141 \\\\\n218\t\\bottomrule\n219\t\\end{tabular}\n220\t\\end{table}\n221\t\n222\t\\subsection{Pretrained LLM Representation Quality Under Visual Input}\n223\t\\label{sec:ood_diagnostics}\n224\t\n225\tA potential concern with the three-condition comparison (\\S\\ref{sec:model_data}) is that the pretrained Llama-2 backbone never encountered projected visual tokens during pretraining, so its representations of such input could be degenerate.\n226\tWe evaluate three diagnostics to assess representation quality.\n227\tActivation magnitude grows from $16.5$ (L0) to $90.2$ (L31) for Llama-2 base, compared to $16.5 \\to 51.7$ for the VLA-trained model; the pretrained LLM produces larger but non-exploding activations with no evidence of collapse.\n228\tPer-layer cosine similarity between L0 and deeper layers decreases progressively from $1.0$ to $\\approx 0.28$ at L25, consistent with gradual differentiation rather than catastrophic out-of-distribution behavior.\n229\tTop-10 principal component variance explained remains stable across all layers ($0.59$--$0.74$), with no low-rank collapse.\n230\tThese diagnostics indicate that the pretrained LLM produces structured, full-rank representations of visual input despite never encountering projected visual tokens during pretraining (Figure~\\ref{fig:ood_diagnostics}). These results support the validity of the three-condition comparison.\n231\t\n232\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n233\t\\centering\n234\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_ood_diagnostics.pdf}\n235\t\\caption{Pretrained LLM representation diagnostics under visual input. Left: activation magnitude across layers (Llama-2 base vs.\\ VLA-trained). Center: cosine similarity between L0 and deeper layers. Right: top-10 PC variance explained. All three metrics indicate structured, non-degenerate representations.}\n236\t\\label{fig:ood_diagnostics}\n237\t\\end{figure}\n238\t\n239\t\\subsection{Probe Architecture Sensitivity}\n240\t\\label{sec:linear_probe}\n241\t\n242\tTo rule out that our findings depend on MLP probe capacity ($N/\\text{params} \\approx 0.06$, overparameterized), we replicate the per-layer analysis with a linear probe ($4{,}096 \\to 7$, $N/\\text{params} \\approx 2.2$).\n243\tThe inverted-U pattern reproduces: trained image\\_mean peaks at L14 ($R^2 = 0.565$), while untrained declines monotonically from L0 ($R^2 = 0.517$).\n244\tThe trained--untrained gap is larger under the linear probe (L14: $\\Delta = +0.324$) than the MLP probe (L8: $\\Delta = +0.015$); VLA training thus reorganizes representations to make action information more linearly decodable.\n245\tUntrained deep layers collapse entirely under the linear probe (L25: $R^2 = -0.298$); MLP nonlinearity partially rescues these degraded representations.\n246\tFor last\\_preaction tokens, the linear probe also corroborates the inverted-U (peak L13, $R^2 = 0.596$), with the same peak layer as the MLP probe and a trained-vs-untrained gap of $\\Delta = 0.103$ ($p < 0.001$).\n247\tThese results indicate that our qualitative findings reflect representational structure rather than probe capacity artifacts (Figure~\\ref{fig:linear_vs_mlp}).\n248\t\n249\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n250\t\\centering\n251\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_linear_vs_mlp_probe.pdf}\n252\t\\caption{Per-layer action probe $R^2$ for linear (dashed) and MLP (solid) probes across trained (blue) and untrained (red) conditions. The inverted-U reproduces under both probe architectures; the trained--untrained gap is larger for linear probes.}\n253\t\\label{fig:linear_vs_mlp}\n254\t\\end{figure}\n255\t\n256\t\\subsection{Functional Probe Comparison}\n257\t\\label{sec:probe_bars_appendix}\n258\t\n259\tFigure~\\ref{fig:probe_bars} compares functional probe $R^2$ across representation sources at each source's best layer.\n260\t\n261\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n262\t\\centering\n263\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig4.pdf}\n264\t\\caption{Functional probe $R^2$ for action prediction (best layer per source). Trained readout ($R^2{=}0.678$, L13) and trained image\\_mean ($0.655$, L8) exceed the projector baseline ($0.630$); the inverted-U extends to functional capacity.}\n265\t\\label{fig:probe_bars}\n266\t\\end{figure}\n267\t\n268\t\\subsection{Training Effect and Instruction Invariance}\n269\t\\label{sec:instruction_invariance_appendix}\n270\t\n271\tFigure~\\ref{fig:factorial} shows trained vs.\\ untrained RSA profiles and instruction invariance.\n272\tInstruction invariance holds across all conditions: maximum $|\\Delta| < 0.006$, so action geometry is visually grounded and not modulated by language instructions.\n273\t\n274\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n275\t\\centering\n276\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig5.pdf}\n277\t\\caption{\\textbf{(a)}~Trained vs.\\ untrained image\\_mean RSA profiles. Training shifts the peak from L0 ($0.612$) to L12 ($0.628$), yielding a modest best-vs-best gain ($\\Delta{=}+0.017$, $3\\%$). \\textbf{(b)}~Instruction invariance: all conditions within $|\\Delta|{<}0.006$; action geometry is visually grounded.}\n278\t\\label{fig:factorial}\n279\t\\end{figure}\n280\t\n281\t\\subsection{Additional RSA Values}\n282\t\\label{sec:additional_rsa}\n283\t\n284\tDINOv2 \\texttt{patch\\_mean} achieves RSA $\\rho = 0.631$, between the \\texttt{cls\\_token} ($0.692$) and the fused DinoSigLIP ($0.634$).\n285\tVLA untrained readout peaks at L1 ($\\rho = 0.626$).\n286\t\n287\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: LIBERO-Spatial}\n288\t\\label{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}\n289\t\n290\tTo assess generalization beyond in-distribution tasks, we repeat the core RSA and probe analyses on LIBERO-Spatial (10 out-of-distribution tasks $\\times$ 50 demonstrations each, requiring spatial reasoning).\n291\tFigure~\\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} shows per-layer RSA and $R^2$ profiles for both LIBERO-Goal and LIBERO-Spatial side by side.\n292\t\n293\tThe inverted-U profile replicates on LIBERO-Spatial: trained image\\_mean RSA peaks at L14 ($\\rho = 0.691$) and $R^2$ at L13 ($0.696$), with peaks shifting ${\\sim}2$ layers deeper than LIBERO-Goal (RSA L12, $R^2$ L8--L10), consistent with increased task difficulty requiring deeper processing.\n294\tTraining produces a significant geometric gain (best-vs-best $\\Delta = +0.023$, 95\\% CI $[0.012, 0.036]$, $p < 0.001$), larger than the LIBERO-Goal effect ($\\Delta = +0.017$, CI includes zero).\n295\t\n296\tTwo notable differences emerge.\n297\tFirst, the absolute RSA values are higher on Spatial ($0.691$ vs.\\ $0.628$), possibly reflecting greater action diversity in spatial tasks that produces a more discriminative action RDM.\n298\tSecond, RSA--$R^2$ peak convergence does not replicate: readout RSA peaks at L15 while $R^2$ peaks at L21 (6-layer gap), contrasting with the near-alignment on Goal ($|\\Delta_\\text{layer}| = 1$).\n299\tThis suggests that peak convergence is benchmark-specific rather than a general consequence of training.\n300\t\n301\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n302\t\\centering\n303\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig7.pdf}\n304\t\\caption{Cross-benchmark validation: per-layer RSA and $R^2$ profiles on LIBERO-Goal (left) vs.\\ LIBERO-Spatial (right). The inverted-U replicates on both benchmarks. LIBERO-Spatial shows a significant training effect (RSA best-vs-best $\\Delta = +0.023$, $p < 0.001$) and deeper peak layers (RSA L14, $R^2$ L13), but RSA--$R^2$ peak convergence does not replicate (6-layer gap).}\n305\t\\label{fig:cross_benchmark}\n306\t\\end{figure}\n307\t\n308\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Benchmark Counterfactual Replication}\n309\t\\label{sec:spatial_counterfactual}\n310\t\n311\tTable~\\ref{tab:counterfactual_spatial} reports counterfactual instruction analysis on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks).\n312\tThe effect ordering is identical to LIBERO-Goal: empty $>$ wrong $>$ shuffled $>$ paraphrased, with the same shuffled--paraphrased violation ($\\rho = 0.8$).\n313\tAll effect sizes exceed $d = 0.8$.\n314\tSpatial instructions differ only in prepositions (e.g., ``on the plate'' vs.\\ ``in the drawer''); the wrong-task $d = 0.851$ demonstrates that instruction-dominant heads encode fine-grained spatial relations, not merely task identity.\n315\t\n316\t\\begin{table}[h]\n317\t\\centering\n318\t\\caption{Counterfactual instruction analysis on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks). Effect ordering replicates LIBERO-Goal (Table~\\ref{tab:counterfactual}), including the shuffled $>$ paraphrased violation. All $d > 0.8$.}\n319\t\\label{tab:counterfactual_spatial}\n320\t\\small\n321\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n322\t\\toprule\n323\tCondition & Jaccard & Cohen's $d$ & CV \\\\\n324\t\\midrule\n325\tEmpty (no instruction) & 0.000 & 0.942 & 0.185 \\\\\n326\tWrong (different task) & 0.174 & 0.851 & 0.093 \\\\\n327\tShuffled (random order) & 1.000 & 0.833 & 0.166 \\\\\n328\tParaphrased & 0.533 & 0.806 & 0.128 \\\\\n329\t\\bottomrule\n330\t\\end{tabular}\n331\t\\end{table}\n332\t\n333\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction Probe on LIBERO-Spatial}\n334\t\\label{sec:spatial_cross_instruction}\n335\t\n336\tTable~\\ref{tab:spatial_cross_instruction} reports cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy on LIBERO-Spatial.\n337\tThe core pattern from LIBERO-Goal replicates: image\\_mean remains at chance (${\\sim}10\\%$) across all layers and conditions, while the trained readout token saturates to $100\\%$ by L4.\n338\tThe untrained readout decays more steeply than on LIBERO-Goal ($75.4\\%$ at L31 vs.\\ $82\\%$), consistent with greater task difficulty.\n339\t\n340\t\\begin{table}[h]\n341\t\\centering\n342\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy on LIBERO-Spatial. Trained readout achieves $100\\%$ from L4; image\\_mean remains at chance. Untrained readout decays more steeply than on LIBERO-Goal (cf.\\ Table~\\ref{tab:dense_routing_main}).}\n343\t\\label{tab:spatial_cross_instruction}\n344\t\\small\n345\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n346\t\\toprule\n347\tToken & Condition & L1 & L4 & L8 & L16 & L31 \\\\\n348\t\\midrule\n349\tlast\\_preaction & Trained & 98.9\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% \\\\\n350\tlast\\_preaction & Untrained & 99.5\\% & 98.3\\% & 85.7\\% & 68.3\\% & 75.4\\% \\\\\n351\timage\\_mean & Trained & 10.2\\% & 10.0\\% & 9.9\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.1\\% \\\\\n352\timage\\_mean & Untrained & 10.1\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.2\\% \\\\\n353\t\\bottomrule\n354\t\\end{tabular}\n355\t\\end{table}\n356\t\n357\t\\subsection{Robustness Analyses}\n358\t\\label{sec:robustness_appendix}\n359\t\n360\t\\subsubsection{Distance Metric Invariance}\n361\t\\label{sec:metric_invariance}\n362\t\n363\tThe inverted-U profile is robust to distance metric choice: cosine action distance yields identical peak layers (L12) and Pearson correlation $r > 0.989$ with the Euclidean profile reported in the main text (Table~\\ref{tab:metric_invariance}).\n364\t\n365\t\\begin{table}[h]\n366\t\\centering\n367\t\\caption{Peak layer comparison between Euclidean and cosine action distances. Profile $r$ denotes Pearson correlation across all 32 layers.}\n368\t\\label{tab:metric_invariance}\n369\t\\small\n370\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n371\t\\toprule\n372\tCondition & Eucl.\\ Peak & Cos.\\ Peak & Profile $r$ \\\\\n373\t\\midrule\n374\tTrained image\\_mean & L12 & L12 & 0.990 \\\\\n375\tTrained last\\_preaction & L12 & L13 & 0.990 \\\\\n376\tUntrained image\\_mean & L0 & L0 & 0.995 \\\\\n377\tUntrained last\\_preaction & L1 & L1 & 0.992 \\\\\n378\t\\bottomrule\n379\t\\end{tabular}\n380\t\\end{table}\n381\t\n382\t\\subsubsection{Per-DOF Decomposition}\n383\t\\label{sec:perdof}\n384\t\n385\tRSA decomposition by action dimension indicates the inverted-U is not driven by any single degree of freedom: xyz translation (peak L13, $\\rho = 0.193$), rotation (peak L12, $\\rho = 0.175$), and gripper (peak L13, $\\rho = 0.106$) all exhibit non-monotonic profiles.\n386\tPer-DOF RSA values are lower than the 7-D RSA because single-dimension distances carry less discriminative information than the full action space.\n387\t\n388\t\\subsubsection{Permutation Test}\n389\t\\label{sec:permutation}\n390\t\n391\tPermutation testing (10{,}000 shuffles) indicates all observed RSA values are highly significant: observed $\\rho$ ($0.42$--$0.63$) exceeds the null distribution maximum ($0.08$--$0.10$) by $15$--$23$ standard deviations (all $p < 0.0001$).\n392\t\n393\t\\subsubsection{Probe Seed Sensitivity}\n394\t\\label{sec:seed_sensitivity}\n395\t\n396\tAction probe $R^2$ is highly stable across random seeds.\n397\tOver 5 seeds (trained image\\_mean, full-layer scan), per-layer $R^2$ standard deviation is uniformly below $0.01$ (mean $0.004$), and the peak $R^2$ varies by less than $0.4\\%$ ($0.655 \\pm 0.002$; Table~\\ref{tab:seed_sensitivity}).\n398\tPeak layer concentrates in the L8--L10 region ($4/5$ seeds); the inverted-U shape is not a seed artifact.\n399\tSeed 1 peaks at L16, but with $R^2$ difference ${<}0.002$. The inverted-U plateau (L8--L16) is sufficiently flat that argmax is sensitive to minor noise while the profile shape is invariant.\n400\tA 15-seed replication confirms stability: trained peak remains at L9 ($R^2 = 0.637 \\pm 0.004$), with all trained/Llama-2-base layers showing CI width ${<}0.02$; untrained exhibits higher variance (std $0.185$ at peak), consistent with random initialization instability.\n401\tFixed-layer analysis at L9 yields $R^2 = 0.637 \\pm 0.008$ (trained) and $0.635 \\pm 0.011$ (Llama-2 base), confirming the inverted-U pattern.\n402\tThe per-seed peak method reports higher absolute values ($0.655$) because each seed selects its optimal layer independently; both methods agree on the qualitative pattern and the trained $>$ untrained ordering.\n403\t\n404\t\\begin{table}[h]\n405\t\\centering\n406\t\\caption{Probe $R^2$ peak across 5 random seeds (trained image\\_mean).}\n407\t\\label{tab:seed_sensitivity}\n408\t\\small\n409\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n410\t\\toprule\n411\tSeed & Peak Layer & Peak $R^2$ \\\\\n412\t\\midrule\n413\t0 & L9 & 0.654 \\\\\n414\t1 & L16 & 0.656 \\\\\n415\t2 & L9 & 0.653 \\\\\n416\t3 & L10 & 0.655 \\\\\n417\t42 & L9 & 0.655 \\\\\n418\t\\midrule\n419\tMean$\\pm$std & $10.6\\pm2.7$ & $0.655\\pm0.002$ \\\\\n420\t\\bottomrule\n421\t\\end{tabular}\n422\t\\end{table}\n423\t\n424\t\\subsubsection{Demonstration Subsample Sensitivity}\n425\t\\label{sec:demo_sensitivity}\n426\t\n427\tTo verify that the inverted-U pattern is not an artifact of sample size, we subsample 25 from 50 demonstrations per task and recompute both RSA and probe profiles (Table~\\ref{tab:demo_sensitivity}).\n428\tThe RSA peak layer remains at L12 with a profile correlation of $r = 0.970$ between 50- and 25-demo conditions (high stability).\n429\tThe probe peak shifts from L9 to L11 (within the L8--L16 plateau) with $r = 0.819$; $R^2$ is more sensitive to training set size than RSA.\n430\tBoth metrics show reduced absolute values (RSA $-3.6\\%$, $R^2$ $-7.6\\%$) as expected from halved data, but the non-monotonic profile shape is preserved.\n431\t\n432\t\\begin{table}[h]\n433\t\\centering\n434\t\\caption{Inverted-U stability under demonstration subsampling (trained image\\_mean).}\n435\t\\label{tab:demo_sensitivity}\n436\t\\small\n437\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n438\t\\toprule\n439\tMetric & 50 demos & 25 demos & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n440\t\\midrule\n441\tRSA peak layer & L12 & L12 & 0 \\\\\n442\tRSA peak $\\rho$ & 0.628 & 0.606 & $-3.6\\%$ \\\\\n443\t$R^2$ peak layer & L9 & L11 & $+2$ \\\\\n444\t$R^2$ peak value & 0.659 & 0.609 & $-7.6\\%$ \\\\\n445\tRSA profile $r$ & --- & 0.970 & --- \\\\\n446\t$R^2$ profile $r$ & --- & 0.819 & --- \\\\\n447\t\\bottomrule\n448\t\\end{tabular}\n449\t\\end{table}\n450\t\n451\t\\subsubsection{Statistical Power}\n452\t\\label{sec:statistical_power}\n453\t\n454\tPost-hoc power analysis ($N = 10$ tasks, $\\alpha = 0.05$, target power $= 0.80$) indicates minimum detectable effect sizes of $\\Delta_{\\text{RSA}} = 0.030$ and $\\Delta_{R^2} = 0.026$.\n455\tThe observed RSA training effect ($\\Delta = 0.017$, Cohen's $d_z = 0.56$) achieves power $= 0.356$, confirming the analysis is underpowered for this effect size; the non-significant RSA result (\\S\\ref{sec:nonmonotonic}) should not be interpreted as evidence of absence.\n456\tThe observed $R^2$ readout effect ($\\Delta = 0.069$, $d_z = 2.68$) achieves power $= 1.000$, confirming adequate sensitivity for the primary functional metric.\n457\t\n458\t\\subsection{Detailed Training Effects}\n459\t\\label{sec:training_effects_appendix}\n460\t\n461\tTable~\\ref{tab:training_effects} reports bootstrap-based statistical comparisons of training effects on RSA and probe $R^2$.\n462\t\n463\t\\begin{table}[h]\n464\t\\centering\n465\t\\caption{Training effects on action-relevant structure (LIBERO-Goal; bootstrap, 10{,}000 resamples). RSA best-vs-best yields a modest geometric gain ($\\Delta{=}+0.017$, $3\\%$; LIBERO-Spatial: $+0.023$, $p{<}0.001$), while $R^2$ shows substantially larger functional gains.}\n466\t\\label{tab:training_effects}\n467\t\\small\n468\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n469\t\\toprule\n470\tComparison & $\\Delta$ & 95\\% CI & $p$ \\\\\n471\t\\midrule\n472\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\emph{RSA ($\\rho$)}} \\\\\n473\tBest-vs-best image\\_mean & $+$0.017 & incl.\\ 0 & n.s. \\\\\n474\t\\quad (trained L12 vs.\\ untrained L0) & & & \\\\\n475\tSame-layer L12 image\\_mean & $+$0.079 & excl.\\ 0 & $<$0.0001 \\\\\n476\t\\quad (trained vs.\\ untrained) & & & \\\\\n477\tInstruction effect (max $|\\Delta|$) & 0.006 & --- & n.s. \\\\\n478\t\\midrule\n479\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\emph{Probe $R^2$}} \\\\\n480\tBest-vs-best image\\_mean & $+$0.015 & excl.\\ 0 & 0.006 \\\\\n481\t\\quad (trained L8 vs.\\ untrained L0) & & & \\\\\n482\tBest-vs-best readout & $+$0.069 & excl.\\ 0 & $<$0.0001 \\\\\n483\t\\quad (trained L13 vs.\\ untrained L32) & & & \\\\\n484\t\\bottomrule\n485\t\\end{tabular}\n486\t\\end{table}\n487\t\n488\t\\subsection{Training Effect Size Summary}\n489\t\\label{sec:training_effect_summary}\n490\t\n491\tTable~\\ref{tab:training_effect_summary} consolidates training effects across all metrics.\n492\tTraining effects on image\\_mean metrics are modest (RSA $\\Delta = +0.017$, $R^2$ $\\Delta = +0.015$), but effects on last\\_preaction metrics are substantial (instruction attention $+18$pp, attention $+46$pp, $R^2$ $\\Delta = +0.069$).\n493\tThis asymmetry indicates that training primarily reshapes how the readout token integrates information rather than altering the visual representations carried by image tokens.\n494\t\n495\t\\begin{table}[h]\n496\t\\centering\n497\t\\caption{Training effect sizes across metrics. Image\\_mean effects are modest; last\\_preaction effects are large. Training targets readout-token information integration.}\n498\t\\label{tab:training_effect_summary}\n499\t\\small\n500\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n501\t\\toprule\n502\tMetric & Trained & Untrained & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n503\t\\midrule\n504\tRSA peak (image\\_mean) & 0.628 (L12) & 0.612 (L0) & $+$0.017 \\\\\n505\t$R^2$ peak (image\\_mean) & 0.655 (L8) & 0.640 (L0) & $+$0.015 \\\\\n506\t$R^2$ peak (last\\_preaction) & 0.678 (L13) & 0.609 (L32) & $+$0.069 \\\\\n507\tInstruction attention L31 & 100\\% & 82\\% & $+$18pp \\\\\n508\tInstruction attention L1 & 58\\% & 12\\% & $+$46pp \\\\\n509\tProgress classification & 86.3\\% (L20) & 85.3\\% (L4) & $+$1pp \\\\\n510\t\\bottomrule\n511\t\\end{tabular}\n512\t\\end{table}\n513\t\n514\t\\subsection{Instruction Classification Probes}\n515\t\\label{sec:routing_appendix}\n516\t\n517\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction Classification: Dense Layer Profile}\n518\t\\label{sec:dense_routing}\n519\t\n520\tTable~\\ref{tab:dense_routing} shows the dense per-layer profile of cross-instruction classification (10-way) at the readout token for layers L0--L8.\n521\tThe step-function onset between L0 ($10\\%$, chance) and L1 (${\\geq}99.6\\%$) reflects the high discriminability of instruction embeddings from the frozen tokenizer: even incidental attention (${\\sim}12\\%$) suffices for linear classification at L1.\n522\tBoth pretrained and VLA-trained models sustain $100\\%$ through L31; only random initialization shows progressive decay ($82\\%$ at L31). Deep-layer stability thus derives from structured pretrained weights rather than task-specific fine-tuning.\n523\t\n524\t\\begin{table}[h]\n525\t\\centering\n526\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy at readout token, L0--L8. Values reflect dense classification evaluation; main-text Table~\\ref{tab:dense_routing_main} reports sparse single-split results, yielding minor differences (e.g., L8: $98.2\\%$ vs.\\ $98.6\\%$).}\n527\t\\label{tab:dense_routing}\n528\t\\small\n529\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n530\t\\toprule\n531\tLayer & Trained & Untrained & Image\\_mean (trained) \\\\\n532\t\\midrule\n533\tL0 & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n534\tL1 & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n535\tL2 & 100.0\\% & 99.6\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n536\tL3 & 100.0\\% & 99.6\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n537\tL4 & 100.0\\% & 99.1\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n538\tL5 & 100.0\\% & 98.2\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n539\tL6 & 100.0\\% & 97.3\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n540\tL7 & 100.0\\% & 96.4\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n541\tL8 & 100.0\\% & 98.6\\% & 10.0\\% \\\\\n542\t\\bottomrule\n543\t\\end{tabular}\n544\t\\end{table}\n545\t\n546\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction LOOCV Validation}\n547\t\\label{sec:loocv}\n548\t\n549\tLeave-one-out cross-validation over 10 folds (each fold holds out one task's instructions) validates that the cross-instruction probe result is not driven by memorization.\n550\tTrained last\\_preaction achieves $100\\% \\pm 0.00$ across all 10 folds at layers L1--L31 (perfect generalization).\n551\t\n552\t\\subsubsection{QA Probe: Progress and Subtask Classification}\n553\t\\label{sec:qa_probe}\n554\t\n555\tTo test whether LLM layers encode task-relevant semantic information beyond instruction identity, we train logistic regression probes (scikit-learn, $C = 1.0$, max\\_iter $= 1{,}000$) to classify task progress (4-class: each trajectory split into 4 equal temporal segments) and active subtask (binary: first vs.\\ second half) from frozen representations, with an 80/20 random train/test split (Table~\\ref{tab:qa_probe}).\n556\t\n557\t\\begin{table}[h]\n558\t\\centering\n559\t\\caption{QA probe accuracy (progress + subtask classification, best layer).}\n560\t\\label{tab:qa_probe}\n561\t\\small\n562\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n563\t\\toprule\n564\tSource & Best Layer & Accuracy \\\\\n565\t\\midrule\n566\tTrained last\\_preaction & L20 & 86.3\\% \\\\\n567\tUntrained last\\_preaction & L20 & 85.3\\% \\\\\n568\tDINOv2 cls\\_token & --- & 82.7\\% \\\\\n569\t\\bottomrule\n570\t\\end{tabular}\n571\t\\end{table}\n572\t\n573\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction Probe Profiles}\n574\t\\label{sec:probe_profiles}\n575\t\n576\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n577\t\\centering\n578\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_probe_conditions.pdf}\n579\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy at the readout token across all 32 LLM layers. VLA-trained achieves $99.6\\%$ at L1, saturating to $100\\%$. Llama-2 base starts at $80.2\\%$, reaching $100\\%$ by L5. Untrained starts at $100\\%$ but decays to $82\\%$ by L31.}\n580\t\\label{fig:probe_conditions_appendix}\n581\t\\end{figure}\n582\t\n583\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Architecture Probe and Attention Profile}\n584\t\\label{app:finetuned_probe}\n585\t\n586\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_probe} reports cross-instruction probe accuracy and L1 instruction-dominant head counts across six conditions spanning two architectures.\n587\tThe probe-attention dissociation at L1 is notable: TraceVLA training \\emph{increases} instruction-dominant heads ($28 \\to 32$) while \\emph{decreasing} probe accuracy ($98.1\\% \\to 83.6\\%$), consistent with representational reorganization for attention efficiency rather than linear separability.\n588\t\n589\t\\begin{table}[h]\n590\t\\centering\n591\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way probe accuracy and L1 instruction-dominant heads across six conditions.\n592\tProbe accuracy and attention head count are dissociated at L1: TracVLA-Phi3V increases heads ($28 \\to 32$) while decreasing probe accuracy ($98.1\\% \\to 83.6\\%$), paralleling the information-computation dissociation observed in OpenVLA.\n593\tImage\\_mean remains at chance ($10\\%$) in all conditions (omitted).}\n594\t\\label{tab:cross_arch_probe}\n595\t\\small\n596\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n597\t\\toprule\n598\tCondition & L1 heads & L1 & L2+ & L31 & Mode \\\\\n599\t\\midrule\n600\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\emph{OpenVLA (Llama-2 7B)}} \\\\\n601\t\\quad Random init & 0/32 & 100.0\\% & decays & 82.0\\% & incid.+forget \\\\\n602\t\\quad Llama-2 base & 9/32 & 80.2\\% & L5: 100\\% & 100.0\\% & gradual+stable \\\\\n603\t\\quad VLA trained & 24/32 & 99.6\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & immed.+stable \\\\\n604\t\\quad LIBERO-finetuned & 22/32 & 99.7\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & immed.+stable \\\\\n605\t\\midrule\n606\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\emph{TraceVLA (Phi-3-V 4B)}} \\\\\n607\t\\quad Phi-3-Instruct & 28/32 & 98.1\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & inherited+stable \\\\\n608\t\\quad VLA trained & 32/32 & 83.6\\% & 100\\% & 100.0\\% & reorg.+stable \\\\\n609\t\\bottomrule\n610\t\\end{tabular}\n611\t\\end{table}\n612\t\n613\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n614\t\\centering\n615\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_cross_architecture_attention.pdf}\n616\t\\caption{Instruction-dominant heads across layers for two architectures (OpenVLA / TraceVLA), each comparing pre-VLA and VLA-trained conditions. Despite opposite starting points---OpenVLA (Llama-2) begins with sparse attention while TraceVLA (Phi-3-V) begins with distributed attention---VLA training drives both to entry-exit concentration (L1 saturation, mid-layer suppression, L29--L31 re-engagement).}\n617\t\\label{fig:cross_arch_attention}\n618\t\\end{figure}\n619\t\n620\t\\subsubsection{L1 Attention Pattern Analysis}\n621\t\\label{sec:attention_pattern}\n622\t\n623\tFigure~\\ref{fig:attention_appendix} shows the per-head L1 attention distribution at the readout token.\n624\tThe three-level gradient (untrained 0/32, Llama-2 base 9/32, VLA-trained 24/32 instruction-dominant heads) shows that language pretraining seeds partial instruction attention and VLA fine-tuning shifts it further toward instruction dominance.\n625\t\n626\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n627\t\\centering\n628\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_l1_stacked_bar.pdf}\n629\t\\caption{L1 attention from the readout token across three conditions, per head (sorted by instruction attention fraction). VLA-trained: 24/32 instruction-dominant heads ($57.7\\%$ text attention). Llama-2 base: 9/32 heads ($36.5\\%$). Untrained: 0/32 heads ($11.7\\%$). See Table~\\ref{tab:routing_gradient} for quantitative comparison.}\n630\t\\label{fig:attention_appendix}\n631\t\\end{figure}\n632\t\n633\t\\subsubsection{Multi-Layer Attention Patterns}\n634\t\\label{sec:multilayer_attention}\n635\t\n636\tTable~\\ref{tab:multilayer_attention} shows a 4-layer subset of the full 32-layer attention profile (Figure~\\ref{fig:attention_profile}, main text).\n637\tThe full profile (Figure~\\ref{fig:multilayer_attention}) reveals three qualitatively distinct patterns: the untrained model maintains 0 instruction-dominant heads at all 32 layers; Llama-2 base shows a mid-depth peak (L9--L16: 15--22 heads), consistent with typical autoregressive LM processing depth; VLA fine-tuning produces an entry-exit pattern (L0--L1: 21--24 heads, L31: 18 heads, intermediate layers 0--11).\n638\t\n639\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n640\t\\centering\n641\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_multi_layer_attn.pdf}\n642\t\\caption{Instruction-dominant heads and mean text attention fraction across layers for three conditions. VLA-trained shows elevated values at L1 and L31; Llama-2 base peaks at L16; untrained remains near zero throughout.}\n643\t\\label{fig:multilayer_attention}\n644\t\\end{figure}\n645\t\n646\t\\begin{table}[h]\n647\t\\centering\n648\t\\caption{Instruction-dominant heads and text attention fraction at four sampled layers (subset of the 32-layer profile in Figure~\\ref{fig:attention_profile}). VLA-trained shows entry-exit concentration (L1, L31); Llama-2 base peaks at mid-depth (L16); untrained shows no instruction attention. Head counts from the 32-layer bulk extraction may differ slightly from dedicated per-layer analyses (Table~\\ref{tab:routing_gradient}) due to threshold boundary effects.}\n649\t\\label{tab:multilayer_attention}\n650\t\\small\n651\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n652\t\\toprule\n653\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{VLA Trained} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Llama-2 base} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Untrained} \\\\\n654\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5} \\cmidrule(lr){6-7}\n655\tLayer & Heads & Text\\% & Heads & Text\\% & Heads & Text\\% \\\\\n656\t\\midrule\n657\tL1 & \\textbf{24/32} & 57.7 & 9/32 & 36.5 & 0/32 & 11.7 \\\\\n658\tL8 & 4/32 & 46.8 & 8/32 & 48.8 & 0/32 & 7.9 \\\\\n659\tL16 & 10/32 & 48.4 & \\textbf{25/32} & 60.2 & 0/32 & 9.2 \\\\\n660\tL31 & \\textbf{18/32} & 56.3 & 8/32 & 34.9 & 0/32 & 7.6 \\\\\n661\t\\bottomrule\n662\t\\end{tabular}\n663\t\\end{table}\n664\t\n665\t\\subsubsection{Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}\n666\t\\label{sec:threshold_sensitivity}\n667\t\n668\tThe main text defines instruction-dominant heads as those allocating $>50\\%$ attention to instruction tokens.\n669\tTable~\\ref{tab:threshold_sensitivity} shows that the three-condition gradient (untrained $<$ Llama-2 base $<$ VLA-trained) holds across all thresholds from 30\\% to 70\\%; the 50\\% threshold is not a boundary-sensitive choice.\n670\t\n671\t\\begin{table}[h]\n672\t\\centering\n673\t\\caption{Number of instruction-dominant heads at L1 under varying attention thresholds. The three-condition ordering is stable across all thresholds.}\n674\t\\label{tab:threshold_sensitivity}\n675\t\\small\n676\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n677\t\\toprule\n678\tThreshold & VLA Trained & Llama-2 base & Untrained \\\\\n679\t\\midrule\n680\t30\\% & 32 & 13 & 0 \\\\\n681\t40\\% & 31 & 10 & 0 \\\\\n682\t50\\% & 24 & 9 & 0 \\\\\n683\t60\\% & 9 & 4 & 0 \\\\\n684\t70\\% & 5 & 3 & 0 \\\\\n685\t\\bottomrule\n686\t\\end{tabular}\n687\t\\end{table}\n688\t\n689\t\\subsection{L1 Attention Decomposition by Head Group}\n690\t\\label{sec:attention_decomp}\n691\t\n692\tTable~\\ref{tab:attention_decomp} decomposes L1 attention mass at the readout token into three token categories for instruction-dominant and control head groups.\n693\t\n694\t\\begin{table}[h]\n695\t\\centering\n696\t\\caption{Attention mass allocation at Layer 1, averaged across 5 samples. Instruction-dominant heads (defined as $>50\\%$ attention to instruction tokens) allocate 17.5 percentage points more attention to instruction tokens than control heads, with corresponding reductions in vision ($-8.3$pp) and BOS/sink ($-9.2$pp) attention.}\n697\t\\label{tab:attention_decomp}\n698\t\\small\n699\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n700\t\\toprule\n701\tHead Group & $N$ & Instruction & Vision & BOS/Other \\\\\n702\t\\midrule\n703\tInstruction-dominant & 24 & 62.4\\% & 18.8\\% & 18.8\\% \\\\\n704\tControl & 8 & 44.9\\% & 27.1\\% & 28.0\\% \\\\\n705\t\\midrule\n706\t$\\Delta$ & --- & $+$17.5pp & $-$8.3pp & $-$9.2pp \\\\\n707\t\\bottomrule\n708\t\\end{tabular}\n709\t\\end{table}\n710\t\n711\tHead-level instruction attention entropy reaches $99.5\\%$ of the uniform maximum; attention is broadly distributed rather than concentrated in a few heads.\n712\t\n713\t\\paragraph{Per-token attention normalization.}\n714\tThe $57.7\\%$ aggregate text attention in instruction-dominant heads partly reflects token-count imbalance (22 instruction vs.\\ 256 image tokens).\n715\tPer-token normalization reveals a $38.2\\times$ ratio: each instruction token receives $38.2\\times$ the attention of each image token in trained instruction-dominant heads, compared to only $1.5\\times$ in untrained heads (a $25.5\\times$ increase from training).\n716\tInstruction-dominant heads also show lower attention entropy ($0.465$) than non-dominant heads ($0.569$), indicating more concentrated attention distributions.\n717\t\n718\t\\subsection{Per-Dimension Action MAE Under Head Ablation}\n719\t\\label{sec:per_dim_mae}\n720\t\n721\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_dim_mae} extends Table~\\ref{tab:action_shift_perdim} with full per-dimension control-head data and instruction-to-control MAE ratios.\n722\t\n723\t\\begin{table}[h]\n724\t\\centering\n725\t\\caption{Mean absolute error per action dimension under L1 head ablation. Translation dimensions ($dx$/$dy$/$dz$) show 3--5$\\times$ larger instruction-to-control ratio than rotation dimensions (1.9--2.6$\\times$); instruction-dominant heads have disproportionate influence on positional control. Gripper (2.07$\\times$) falls between the two groups.}\n726\t\\label{tab:per_dim_mae}\n727\t\\small\n728\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n729\t\\toprule\n730\tDimension & Instr.\\ 24h MAE & Control 8h MAE & Ratio \\\\\n731\t\\midrule\n732\t$dx$ & 0.00189 & 0.00037 & 5.09$\\times$ \\\\\n733\t$dy$ & 0.00531 & 0.00125 & 4.26$\\times$ \\\\\n734\t$dz$ & 0.00762 & 0.00243 & 3.14$\\times$ \\\\\n735\t$roll$ & 0.02059 & 0.00785 & 2.62$\\times$ \\\\\n736\t$pitch$ & 0.03071 & 0.01387 & 2.21$\\times$ \\\\\n737\t$yaw$ & 0.03388 & 0.01783 & 1.90$\\times$ \\\\\n738\t$gripper$ & 0.29072 & 0.14072 & 2.07$\\times$ \\\\\n739\t\\bottomrule\n740\t\\end{tabular}\n741\t\\end{table}\n742\t\n743\t\\subsection{Per-Task $R^2$ Distribution Under L1 Ablation}\n744\t\\label{sec:per_task_r2}\n745\t\n746\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_task_r2} reports per-task action probe $R^2$ at L1 under baseline and 24-head ablation conditions (MLP probe, 5 demonstrations per task).\n747\t\n748\t\\begin{table}[h]\n749\t\\centering\n750\t\\caption{Per-task action probe $R^2$ at L1 (MLP probe, 5 demos/task). All 10 tasks show positive ablation effect ($\\Delta > 0$): instruction-head ablation uniformly increases action decodability at the entry layer. Mean $\\Delta R^2 = +0.107$; the 50-demo setting (\\S\\ref{sec:learned_routing}) yields a consistent $+0.059$.}\n751\t\\label{tab:per_task_r2}\n752\t\\small\n753\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n754\t\\toprule\n755\tTask & Baseline $R^2$ & Ablated $R^2$ & $\\Delta R^2$ \\\\\n756\t\\midrule\n757\topen middle drawer & 0.197 & 0.221 & $+$0.024 \\\\\n758\topen top drawer + bowl & 0.265 & 0.340 & $+$0.075 \\\\\n759\tpush plate to stove & 0.190 & 0.197 & $+$0.007 \\\\\n760\tput bowl on plate & 0.375 & 0.474 & $+$0.099 \\\\\n761\tput bowl on stove & 0.235 & 0.395 & $+$0.160 \\\\\n762\tput bowl on cabinet & 0.256 & 0.457 & $+$0.201 \\\\\n763\tput cream cheese in bowl & 0.186 & 0.345 & $+$0.159 \\\\\n764\tput wine on rack & 0.189 & 0.424 & $+$0.235 \\\\\n765\tput wine on cabinet & 0.279 & 0.353 & $+$0.074 \\\\\n766\tturn on stove & 0.385 & 0.421 & $+$0.036 \\\\\n767\t\\midrule\n768\tMean $\\pm$ std & $0.256 \\pm 0.070$ & $0.363 \\pm 0.088$ & $+$0.107 \\\\\n769\t\\bottomrule\n770\t\\end{tabular}\n771\t\\end{table}\n772\t\n773\t\\subsection{Task Complexity and Attention Dependence}\n774\t\\label{sec:task_complexity}\n775\t\n776\tWe classify tasks as \\emph{instruction-discriminative} when multiple tasks share the same manipulated object(s), requiring instruction content to disambiguate the target action (e.g., ``put bowl on plate'' vs.\\ ``put bowl on stove''); remaining tasks involve unique object-action pairings recoverable from visual context alone.\n777\tTable~\\ref{tab:task_complexity} reports per-task $|\\Delta R^2|$ under both groupings.\n778\t\n779\t\\begin{table}[h]\n780\t\\centering\n781\t\\caption{Task-level L1 ablation effects ($|\\Delta R^2|$) grouped by instruction dependence. \\emph{Primary} (conservative): cream cheese in bowl classified as visual-sufficient ($2.5\\times$, $p = 0.033$). \\emph{Sensitivity} (shared-object criterion): cream cheese reclassified as instruction-discriminative ($6.5\\times$, $p = 0.008$).}\n782\t\\label{tab:task_complexity}\n783\t\\small\n784\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n785\t\\toprule\n786\tTask & $|\\Delta R^2|$ & Group \\\\\n787\t\\midrule\n788\tput wine on rack & 0.235 & Instr-disc \\\\\n789\tput bowl on cabinet & 0.201 & Instr-disc \\\\\n790\tput bowl on stove & 0.160 & Instr-disc \\\\\n791\tput bowl on plate & 0.099 & Instr-disc \\\\\n792\topen top drawer + bowl & 0.075 & Instr-disc \\\\\n793\tput wine on cabinet & 0.074 & Instr-disc \\\\\n794\t\\midrule\n795\tput cream cheese in bowl$^\\dagger$ & 0.159 & Vis-suff \\\\\n796\tturn on stove & 0.036 & Vis-suff \\\\\n797\topen middle drawer & 0.024 & Vis-suff \\\\\n798\tpush plate to stove & 0.007 & Vis-suff \\\\\n799\t\\midrule\n800\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textbf{Primary}: Instr-disc mean $0.141$; Vis-suff mean $0.057$} \\\\\n801\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{Mann-Whitney $U = 21.0$, $p = 0.033$, ratio $= 2.49\\times$} \\\\\n802\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textbf{Sensitivity}$^\\dagger$: cream cheese as Instr-disc: $p = 0.008$, $6.5\\times$} \\\\\n803\t\\bottomrule\n804\t\\end{tabular}\n805\t\\end{table}\n806\t\n807\t\\paragraph{Leave-one-out sensitivity.}\n808\tAll leave-one-out $p$-values remain $\\leq 0.083$ (marginally significant), with no single task removal reversing the effect direction.\n809\tReclassifying the boundary case (cream cheese in bowl) to the instruction-discriminative group strengthens the result ($p = 0.008$).\n810\t\n811\tBootstrap 95\\% CI on the complex/simple ratio: $[1.03, 11.12]$ (10{,}000 resamples). The wide interval reflects low statistical power from $N=10$ tasks split into groups of 6 and 4; the point estimate ($2.5\\times$) persists under leave-one-out analysis (all $p \\leq 0.083$, marginally significant; \\S\\ref{sec:task_complexity}).\n812\t\n813\t\\subsection{Specificity Control: Matched-Size Head Ablation}\n814\t\\label{sec:specificity_control}\n815\t\n816\tTo test whether ablation effects are specific to instruction-dominant heads, we ablate four matched-size subsets of 24 heads at L1: instruction-dominant (highest instruction attention fraction), image-dominant (highest image attention fraction), random (5 seeds), and bottom-24 (lowest instruction attention fraction; overlaps 16/24 heads with the instruction-dominant group).\n817\t\n818\t\\begin{table}[h]\n819\t\\centering\n820\t\\caption{Matched-size ablation controls: trajectory disruption ($d$) from ablating 24-head subsets at L1, ranked by instruction attention fraction. Instruction-dominant heads produce the largest effect, but the modest gap ($8\\%$ over random) indicates distributed, attention-graded redistribution rather than a dedicated subset.}\n821\t\\label{tab:specificity_control}\n822\t\\small\n823\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n824\t\\toprule\n825\tCondition (24 heads) & Cohen's $d$ & 95\\% CI \\\\\n826\t\\midrule\n827\tInstruction-dominant & 0.650 & (reference) \\\\\n828\tRandom (5 seeds) & $0.601 \\pm 0.021$ & $[0.568, 0.622]$ \\\\\n829\tImage-dominant & 0.580 & $[0.547, 0.613]$ \\\\\n830\tBottom-24 & 0.565 & --- \\\\\n831\t\\bottomrule\n832\t\\end{tabular}\n833\t\\end{table}\n834\t\n835\tAll conditions yield comparable ground-truth MSE ($0.487$--$0.504$ vs.\\ baseline $0.497$), consistent with direction-selective rather than precision-degrading effects.\n836\t\n837\t\\subsection{Paired Effect Sizes}\n838\t\\label{sec:paired_effect_sizes}\n839\t\n840\tMain-text Cohen's $d$ values use the standard (unpaired) formula for token-level comparisons where observations are independent tokens.\n841\tFor within-subject effects where the same tasks or demonstrations are measured under two conditions, we additionally report paired $d_z = \\bar{\\Delta} / s_\\Delta$, which accounts for within-subject correlation and yields larger effect sizes when correlation is high (Table~\\ref{tab:paired_dz}).\n842\t\n843\t\\begin{table}[h]\n844\t\\centering\n845\t\\caption{Paired effect sizes ($d_z$) for within-subject comparisons. Standard (unpaired) $d$ reported where available. Paired $d_z$ exceeds standard $d$ when within-subject correlation is high.}\n846\t\\label{tab:paired_dz}\n847\t\\small\n848\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n849\t\\toprule\n850\tComparison & Standard $d$ & Paired $d_z$ & $N$ \\\\\n851\t\\midrule\n852\tL1 ablation: 24 dominant vs.\\ baseline & 0.65 & 1.17 & 10 tasks \\\\\n853\tRandom-24 ablation vs.\\ baseline & --- & $1.84 \\pm 0.33$ & 30 subsets \\\\\n854\tDose-response: 32 vs.\\ 4 heads (MSE) & --- & 1.79 & 10 tasks \\\\\n855\tRSA: trained vs.\\ untrained (best-vs-best) & --- & 0.55 & 10 tasks \\\\\n856\tCross-arch: OpenVLA vs.\\ TraceVLA peak & --- & 0.48 & 15 seeds \\\\\n857\t\\bottomrule\n858\t\\end{tabular}\n859\t\\end{table}\n860\t\n861\t\\subsection{Cross-Architecture Action Decodability: $2 \\times 2$ Design}\n862\t\\label{sec:cross_arch_r2}\n863\t\n864\tTable~\\ref{tab:2x2_r2} reports per-layer action prediction $R^2$ across two architectures (OpenVLA, TraceVLA) $\\times$ two training conditions (VLA-trained, untrained base LLM), providing a $2 \\times 2$ factorial view of how VLA training reshapes per-layer action decodability.\n865\t\n866\t\\paragraph{Convergent reorganization from divergent starting points.}\n867\tUntrained profiles differ qualitatively: OpenVLA (Llama-2) peaks at L0 ($R^2 = 0.629$) then collapses to near-zero at mid-layers (L20: $0.086$, std $= 0.311$), while Phi-3-V peaks at L6 ($0.578 \\pm 0.018$) with gradual monotonic decline (late-layer std $= 0.068$).\n868\tAfter VLA training, both architectures reach nearly identical mid-layer peaks---OpenVLA at L9 ($R^2 = 0.637 \\pm 0.008$), TraceVLA at L11 ($0.634 \\pm 0.009$)---with $\\Delta = 0.003$ and overlapping CIs.\n869\tDeep-layer variance drops sharply with training: OpenVLA std $0.277 \\to 0.040$; Phi-3-V std $0.068 \\to 0.011$.\n870\tPeak $R^2$ values are comparable before and after training (untrained peaks reach $91$--$99\\%$ of trained), but peak locations shift deeper (L0$\\to$L9; L6$\\to$L11) and profile shapes transform from architecture-specific patterns to a shared mid-layer plateau.\n871\t\n872\t\\paragraph{Summary.}\n873\tTable~\\ref{tab:2x2_r2_summary} condenses the key contrasts.\n874\tThe L0 values are identical between trained and untrained within each architecture (shared frozen vision encoder + projector), confirming that training effects arise from LLM weight changes.\n875\tThe largest training gains appear at mid-to-deep layers (L15--L25 average: OpenVLA $+0.313$, Phi-3-V $+0.103$), precisely where untrained profiles diverge most.\n876\t\n877\t\\begin{table}[h]\n878\t\\centering\n879\t\\caption{$R^2$ profile summary across architectures and training conditions.}\n880\t\\label{tab:2x2_r2_summary}\n881\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n882\t\\toprule\n883\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{TraceVLA (Phi-3-mini)} \\\\\n884\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n885\t & Untrained & Trained & Untrained & Trained \\\\\n886\t\\midrule\n887\tPeak Layer       & L0          & L9          & L6          & L11         \\\\\n888\tPeak $R^2$       & 0.629       & 0.637       & 0.578       & 0.634       \\\\\n889\tProfile Shape    & Collapse    & Flat plateau & Ramp $\\to$ decline & Ramp $\\to$ plateau \\\\\n890\tLate-layer std   & 0.277       & 0.040       & 0.068       & 0.011       \\\\\n891\t$\\Delta_{\\text{train}}$ (peak) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$+0.008$ (L0$\\to$L9)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$+0.056$ (L6$\\to$L11)} \\\\\n892\t$\\Delta_{\\text{train}}$ (L15--L25 avg) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$+0.313$} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$+0.103$} \\\\\n893\t\\bottomrule\n894\t\\end{tabular}\n895\t\\end{table}\n896\t\n897\t\\begin{table}[h]\n898\t\\centering\n899\t\\caption{Per-layer action prediction $R^2$: training effect across architectures (mean $\\pm$ std over 15 seeds). Peak values per condition in \\textbf{bold}.}\n900\t\\label{tab:2x2_r2}\n901\t\\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n902\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n903\t\\toprule\n904\tLayer & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{TraceVLA (Phi-3-mini)} \\\\\n905\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n906\t & Untrained & Trained & Untrained & Trained \\\\\n907\t\\midrule\n908\tL0  & $\\mathbf{0.629 \\pm 0.016}$ & $0.629 \\pm 0.016$ & $-0.330 \\pm 0.003$ & $-0.330 \\pm 0.002$ \\\\\n909\tL1  & $0.539 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.625 \\pm 0.017$ & $0.429 \\pm 0.034$ & $0.447 \\pm 0.013$ \\\\\n910\tL2  & $0.517 \\pm 0.047$ & $0.622 \\pm 0.010$ & $0.458 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.522 \\pm 0.014$ \\\\\n911\tL3  & $0.448 \\pm 0.216$ & $0.624 \\pm 0.014$ & $0.498 \\pm 0.036$ & $0.579 \\pm 0.011$ \\\\\n912\tL4  & $0.496 \\pm 0.057$ & $0.628 \\pm 0.014$ & $0.572 \\pm 0.021$ & $0.592 \\pm 0.009$ \\\\\n913\tL5  & $0.475 \\pm 0.101$ & $0.627 \\pm 0.019$ & $0.573 \\pm 0.020$ & $0.602 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n914\tL6  & $0.422 \\pm 0.119$ & $0.631 \\pm 0.012$ & $\\mathbf{0.578 \\pm 0.018}$ & $0.609 \\pm 0.012$ \\\\\n915\tL7  & $0.459 \\pm 0.093$ & $0.632 \\pm 0.012$ & $0.569 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.616 \\pm 0.014$ \\\\\n916\tL8  & $0.347 \\pm 0.283$ & $0.635 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.560 \\pm 0.025$ & $0.615 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n917\tL9  & $0.445 \\pm 0.127$ & $\\mathbf{0.637 \\pm 0.008}$ & $0.564 \\pm 0.021$ & $0.617 \\pm 0.013$ \\\\\n918\tL10 & $0.353 \\pm 0.224$ & $0.624 \\pm 0.014$ & $0.551 \\pm 0.027$ & $0.625 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n919\tL11 & $0.356 \\pm 0.122$ & $0.623 \\pm 0.016$ & $0.561 \\pm 0.025$ & $\\mathbf{0.634 \\pm 0.009}$ \\\\\n920\tL12 & $0.409 \\pm 0.203$ & $0.626 \\pm 0.011$ & $0.557 \\pm 0.026$ & $0.632 \\pm 0.005$ \\\\\n921\tL13 & $0.341 \\pm 0.227$ & $0.624 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.547 \\pm 0.028$ & $0.633 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n922\tL14 & $0.374 \\pm 0.211$ & $0.623 \\pm 0.015$ & $0.550 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.631 \\pm 0.009$ \\\\\n923\tL15 & $0.297 \\pm 0.225$ & $0.619 \\pm 0.012$ & $0.550 \\pm 0.022$ & $0.633 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n924\tL16 & $0.323 \\pm 0.226$ & $0.619 \\pm 0.011$ & $0.534 \\pm 0.018$ & $0.631 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n925\tL17 & $0.395 \\pm 0.109$ & $0.609 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.519 \\pm 0.024$ & $0.633 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n926\tL18 & $0.214 \\pm 0.307$ & $0.613 \\pm 0.017$ & $0.502 \\pm 0.047$ & $0.629 \\pm 0.012$ \\\\\n927\tL19 & $0.241 \\pm 0.258$ & $0.613 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.499 \\pm 0.058$ & $0.627 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n928\tL20 & $0.086 \\pm 0.311$ & $0.611 \\pm 0.014$ & $0.482 \\pm 0.048$ & $0.628 \\pm 0.011$ \\\\\n929\tL21 & $0.330 \\pm 0.216$ & $0.589 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.476 \\pm 0.036$ & $0.624 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n930\tL22 & $0.207 \\pm 0.283$ & $0.586 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.408 \\pm 0.140$ & $0.616 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n931\tL23 & $0.264 \\pm 0.227$ & $0.578 \\pm 0.028$ & $0.455 \\pm 0.074$ & $0.613 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n932\tL24 & $0.173 \\pm 0.275$ & $0.577 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.446 \\pm 0.062$ & $0.610 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n933\tL25 & $0.130 \\pm 0.305$ & $0.553 \\pm 0.053$ & $0.479 \\pm 0.039$ & $0.609 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n934\tL26 & $0.251 \\pm 0.254$ & $0.535 \\pm 0.046$ & $0.411 \\pm 0.098$ & $0.611 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n935\tL27 & $0.099 \\pm 0.278$ & $0.552 \\pm 0.027$ & $0.366 \\pm 0.197$ & $0.604 \\pm 0.011$ \\\\\n936\tL28 & $0.099 \\pm 0.307$ & $0.544 \\pm 0.034$ & $0.358 \\pm 0.218$ & $0.596 \\pm 0.015$ \\\\\n937\tL29 & $0.088 \\pm 0.275$ & $0.530 \\pm 0.034$ & $0.356 \\pm 0.271$ & $0.585 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n938\tL30 & $0.220 \\pm 0.234$ & $0.511 \\pm 0.029$ & $0.424 \\pm 0.104$ & $0.574 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n939\tL31 & $0.069 \\pm 0.284$ & $0.488 \\pm 0.054$ & $0.332 \\pm 0.262$ & $0.557 \\pm 0.016$ \\\\\n940\tL32 & $0.584 \\pm 0.011$ & $0.535 \\pm 0.015$ & $0.410 \\pm 0.185$ & $0.550 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n941\t\\bottomrule\n942\t\\end{tabular}%\n943\t}\n944\t\\end{table}\n945\t\n946\t\\paragraph{Equivalence testing.}\n947\t\\label{sec:equivalence_testing}\n948\tTo formally test whether the cross-architecture peak $R^2$ values are statistically equivalent (rather than merely failing to differ), we apply the Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) procedure~\\cite{lakens2017equivalence} with equivalence bound $\\varepsilon = 0.05$.\n949\tThe observed difference is $\\Delta = 0.003$ (OpenVLA L9: $0.637 \\pm 0.008$; TraceVLA L11: $0.634 \\pm 0.009$).\n950\tBoth one-sided tests reject at $p < 0.0001$; the 90\\% confidence interval for the difference is $[-0.002, 0.009]$, falling entirely within $[-0.05, 0.05]$.\n951\tA conventional two-sample $t$-test yields $p = 0.318$ (not significant), illustrating the distinction between absence of evidence for a difference and evidence of equivalence: the TOST procedure provides the latter.\n952\t\n953\t\\subsection{Global Multiple Comparison Correction}\n954\t\\label{sec:bonferroni_correction}\n955\t\n956\tThe paper reports 63 statistical tests spanning permutation tests, $t$-tests, bootstrap comparisons, TOST equivalence, and Spearman correlations.\n957\tWe apply a Bonferroni correction ($\\alpha_{\\text{adj}} = 0.05 / 63 = 7.94 \\times 10^{-4}$) and the less conservative Holm--Bonferroni step-down procedure; both yield identical results.\n958\tOf the 45 originally significant tests, 40 survive correction (88.9\\%).\n959\t\n960\tAll eight core tests supporting the paper's main claims remain significant:\n961\tRSA permutation at L12 ($p < 0.0001$), trained vs.\\ untrained image geometry at L12 ($p = 3.9 \\times 10^{-5}$), counterfactual wrong and empty instruction ($p < 10^{-100}$), TOST cross-architecture equivalence ($p < 0.0001$), dose-response top-24 ablation ($p < 10^{-100}$), trained vs.\\ untrained probe $R^2$ ($p < 0.0001$), and bottom-24 specificity ($p < 10^{-90}$).\n962\t\n963\tFive auxiliary tests lose significance under correction:\n964\t(i)~RSA trained vs.\\ untrained preaction at L12 ($p = 0.005 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 0.284$),\n965\t(ii)~DINOv2 vs.\\ SigLIP RSA ($p = 0.032 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 1.0$),\n966\t(iii)~fused backbone vs.\\ SigLIP RSA ($p = 0.011 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 0.673$),\n967\t(iv)~CV exact paired permutation ($p = 0.016 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 0.984$),\n968\tand (v)~$R^2$ projector vs.\\ VLA Spearman correlation ($p = 0.003 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 0.183$).\n969\tThese concern auxiliary comparisons (encoder ranking, coefficient-of-variation ordering) rather than the core redistribution, probing, or counterfactual findings; no main-text conclusion requires revision.\n970\t\n971\t\\subsection{Mechanistic Interpretation}\n972\t\\label{sec:mechanistic_interpretation}\n973\t\n974\tMatched-size ablation ($d$: instruction $0.65$, random $0.60$, image $0.58$; Table~\\ref{tab:specificity_control}) and dose-response (steep through 12 heads, plateau beyond 20) indicate distributed attention redistribution.\n975\tCounterfactual ordering largely follows token novelty ($\\rho = 0.8$; \\S\\ref{sec:learned_routing}), with one violation: shuffled tokens exceed paraphrased ($d = 0.763$ vs.\\ $0.707$) despite identical token sets, indicating positional sensitivity.\n976\tThat ablation raises $R^2$ yet alters trajectories ($d = 0.65$) without increasing ground-truth error ($\\Delta\\text{MSE} \\approx 0$; \\S\\ref{sec:action_trajectory}) reflects an information-computation dissociation~\\cite{hewitt2019control}: instruction-dominant heads control direction rather than precision. Random ablation yields larger $R^2$ gains ($+0.071$ vs.\\ $+0.030$; $z = -3.11$, $0$\\textsuperscript{th} percentile), consistent with distinct $R^2$ signatures.\n977\t\n978\t\\subsection{Functional Stages and Predictions}\n979\t\\label{sec:functional_stages}\n980\t\n981\tPer-layer profiles suggest three stages: redistribution (L0--L1; probe $99.6\\%$, 24/32 instruction-dominant heads), computation (L8--L13; $R^2$ peak $0.655$--$0.678$), and decay (L14--L31, ${\\sim}56\\%$ of parameters; declining $R^2$ and RSA suggest pruning potential).\n982\tTask-specific fine-tuning eliminates L31 re-engagement (18 $\\to$ 0 heads; \\S\\ref{sec:learned_routing}), consistent with exit-layer attention serving multi-task flexibility ($N{=}2$).\n983\tTraceVLA~\\cite{zheng2024tracevla} (Phi-3-V) confirms consistent entry-exit attention concentration from an opposite starting point (\\S\\ref{sec:learned_routing}).\n984\tThe $2 {\\times} 2$ trained-vs-untrained comparison reveals a consistent reorganization pattern: untrained OpenVLA collapses from L0 ($R^2 = 0.629$) to near-zero at mid-layers (L20: $0.086$, std $= 0.277$), while untrained Phi-3-V peaks at L6 ($0.578$) with gradual decline (std $= 0.068$)---qualitatively opposite untrained landscapes.\n985\tYet training drives both to nearly identical mid-layer peaks ($0.637$ vs.\\ $0.634$, $\\Delta = 0.003$) with stabilized deep-layer variance (OpenVLA std $0.277 \\to 0.040$; Phi-3-V $0.068 \\to 0.011$).\n986\tThis consistent outcome from divergent starting points suggests that mid-layer action specialization is a consistent convergence pattern of VLA training, not an architectural artifact.\n987\tExit-layer $R^2$ differs ($0.488$ vs.\\ $0.557$), possibly reflecting architectural differences in late-layer processing.\n988\tRemaining predictions: cross-attention VLAs ($\\pi_0$~\\cite{black2024_pi0}, Octo~\\cite{ghosh2024_octo}) should lack L1 instruction-dominant heads; larger VLAs should yield flatter per-head contributions.\n989\tFor deployments, L1 instruction probes can flag instruction-discriminative tasks and L1 attention entropy can monitor instruction under-attendance at runtime.\n990\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XCAbMmQAGqoFNBwh8Xxaok","session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3","uuid":"605efff9-de0b-4ebf-8fa9-aecadff772e6","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:22:55.023Z"}
