{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013gv6bpde5u8AoGnQysCfMD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016QbNEcBsaicuDKEkbxmPTe","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"vla-ssd","file":"literature_map.yaml","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1448,"cache_read_input_tokens":15749,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1448,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":53,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fkf1rVNGL1iMs2op5SNsMN","session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3","uuid":"73117ef6-e57e-43fb-bb9e-095c66259f14"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016QbNEcBsaicuDKEkbxmPTe","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"papers:\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # DIRECT COMPETITORS — VLA Representation Analysis / Interpretability\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: grant2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Not All Features Are Created Equal: A Mechanistic Study of Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Bryce Grant, Xijia Zhao, Peng Wang\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint (under review)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.19233\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: competitor\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Visual pathway dominates action generation across all 6 tested VLA architectures (80M–7B params, 394K+ rollout episodes, 4 benchmarks).\\\\n      Language sensitivity depends on task structure: when visual context uniquely specifies the task, language is IGNORED;\\\\n      when multiple goals share a scene, language becomes essential (\\\\\\\"encoded but not used\\\\\\\").\\\\n      Cross-task activation injection steers robots toward source-task positions (99.8% trajectory alignment with source in X-VLA).\\\\n      Multi-pathway architectures (pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T) separate motor programs (expert pathway) from goal semantics (VLM pathway).\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Most direct competitor. Behavioral intervention (activation injection, SAE, probing) reveals vision dominance + language encoded but not used.\\\\n      Uses OpenVLA-OFT checkpoint; we use standard fine-tuning — must qualify this difference in Related Work.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No trained vs untrained representation analysis. They compare different model architectures,\\\\n      we compare trained vs untrained representation structure within the same architecture.\\\\n      Their \\\\\\\"encoded but not used\\\\\\\" is a behavioral-level conclusion; we demonstrate at the representation level\\\\n      that instruction conditioning comes from the input pathway, not training. Complementary work.\\\\n    notes: >\\\\n      Action Atlas interactive exploration tool released. 6 models: OpenVLA, X-VLA, pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T, Octo.\\\\n      OFT checkpoint difference may affect direct comparability.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: swann2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Sparse Autoencoders Reveal Interpretable and Steerable Features in VLA Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Aiden Swann, Lachlain McGranahan, Hugo Buurmeijer, Monroe Kennedy III, Mac Schwager\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.19183\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: competitor\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      SAE features on OpenVLA: majority of extracted features correspond to memorized training sequences,\\\\n      but some correspond to interpretable, general, and steerable motion primitives and semantic properties.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Also OpenVLA interpretability work. They use SAE for feature-level decomposition,\\\\n      we use probing + RSA for representation-level analysis. Methodologically complementary.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No language features analysis. No trained vs untrained comparison.\\\\n      Does not investigate source of instruction conditioning (input pathway vs training).\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Stanford team. Published on arXiv same day as Grant 2026 (2026-03-19).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rizzardo2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Observing and Controlling Features in Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Rizzardo et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.05487\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: competitor\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Introduces feature-observability and feature-controllability concepts bridging LLM mechanistic\\\\n      interpretability and VLAs. Linear classifier observation + optimal-control-based minimal intervention steering.\\\\n      Validated on pi0.5 and OpenVLA — targeted interventions steer robot behavior while preserving closed-loop capabilities.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Also VLA internal representation analysis. They focus on how to steer,\\\\n      we focus on where conditioning comes from (input pathway vs training).\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison. No instruction pathway vs training separation analysis.\\\\n      Focuses on controllability rather than representation structure origin.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2026-03-05.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: haon2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Mechanistic Interpretability for Steering Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Bear Haon, Kaylene Stocking, Ian Chuang, Claire Tomlin\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"CoRL 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2509.00328\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      First VLA mechanistic interpretability framework. Projects feedforward activations onto token embedding basis,\\\\n      identifies sparse semantic directions (speed, direction) causally linked to action selection.\\\\n      Activation steering modulates behavior in real time without fine-tuning.\\\\n      Validated on Pi0 and OpenVLA in LIBERO (simulation) and UR5 (real robot).\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Methodological reference: their activation steering proves VLAs have interpretable semantic directions.\\\\n      Our RSA/probing results can dialogue with their mechanistic findings.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison. No systematic representation structure analysis (RSA/CKA).\\\\n      Focuses on individual semantic direction causal intervention, not overall structure comparison.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"CoRL 2025 accepted. Pioneering work in VLA interpretability.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: molinari2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Emergent World Representations in OpenVLA\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Marco Molinari, Leonardo Nevali, Saharsha Navani, Omar G. Younis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2509.24559\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      OpenVLA implicitly learns a world model. Embedding arithmetic tests state transition knowledge;\\\\n      linear/non-linear probes show statistically significant state transition prediction ability across layers.\\\\n      SAE pipeline analyzes fine-grained world model structure.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Also OpenVLA probing work. They study world model, we study instruction conditioning.\\\\n      Their layer-wise probe results can be compared with our layer-wise RSA.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No language/instruction analysis. No trained vs untrained comparison.\\\\n      Only focuses on state transitions, not goal/instruction representation.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-09-29. Has OpenReview discussion.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: lu2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Probing a Vision-Language-Action Model for Symbolic States and Integration into a Cognitive Architecture\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Hong Lu, Hengxu Li, Prithviraj Singh Shahani, Stephanie Herbers, Matthias Scheutz\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2502.04558\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Probes OpenVLA hidden layers to extract symbolic representations (object properties, relations, action states).\\\\n      LIBERO-spatial pick-and-place probing accuracy > 0.90 across most layers.\\\\n      Integration with cognitive architecture improves interpretability and robustness.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Directly related methodology: both use linear probes on OpenVLA layers on LIBERO.\\\\n      Their finding that symbolic states are decodable (>0.90) is consistent with our goal classification at 100%.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      Only trained model probing, no untrained control.\\\\n      Only LIBERO-spatial single task, no cross-task/cross-condition analysis.\\\\n      No instruction conditioning source analysis.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-02-06. Tufts University.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fang_intention2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"From Intention to Execution: Probing the Generalization Boundaries of Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Irving Fang, Juexiao Zhang, Shengbang Tong, Chen Feng\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2506.09930\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Unified probing suite (50 simulation tasks, 10 subcategories) evaluating VLA generalization boundaries.\\\\n      VLM backbone provides robust perceptual understanding + high-level planning (good intentions),\\\\n      but cannot reliably translate to precise motor execution (perception-to-action gap).\\\\n      Action data fine-tuning erodes original VLM generalist reasoning abilities.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      \\\\\\\"Fine-tuning erodes VLM reasoning\\\\\\\" echoes our \\\\\\\"training doesn't improve representation\\\\\\\" finding.\\\\n      They discover intention != execution at behavioral level; we discover\\\\n      training ~= untrained in representation structure.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      Behavioral-level probing (success rates), not representation-level analysis.\\\\n      No trained vs untrained representation structure comparison.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-06-11. INT-ACT benchmark open-sourced. NYU/ai4ce team.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # DIRECT COMPETITORS — Language Grounding / Counterfactual\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: fang_cf2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"When Vision Overrides Language: Evaluating and Mitigating Counterfactual Failures in VLAs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yu Fang, Yuchun Feng, Dong Jing, Jiaqi Liu, Yue Yang, Zhenyu Wei, Daniel Szafir, Mingyu Ding\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2602.17659\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: competitor\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Introduces LIBERO-CF counterfactual benchmark. VLAs exhibit severe counterfactual failures:\\\\n      vision shortcuts + dataset biases cause models to ignore language intent.\\\\n      OpenVLA-OFT: 4.7% grounding rate, 0.4% success rate.\\\\n      Best model: 30.8% grounding rate, 13.2% success rate.\\\\n      Proposes Counterfactual Action Guidance (CAG) dual-branch inference scheme.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Directly validates \\\\\\\"language encoded but not used\\\\\\\" severity under counterfactual settings.\\\\n      Their behavioral findings complement our representation-level findings.\\\\n      They test OpenVLA-OFT — same checkpoint issue as Grant 2026.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      Pure behavioral benchmark, no representation analysis.\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison. No instruction pathway causal analysis.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2026-02-19. LIBERO-CF has 4 sub-suites (CF-Spatial, CF-Object, CF-Long, CF-OOD).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: hou2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LangGap: Diagnosing and Closing the Language Gap in Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yuchen Hou, Lin Zhao\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.00592\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      SOTA VLAs achieve >95% on standard benchmarks but largely ignore language instructions.\\\\n      Four-dimensional semantic perturbation method exposes language understanding deficits.\\\\n      LIBERO assigns only 1 task per layout, so models dont need to understand language.\\\\n      LangGap benchmark forces multi-task shared layouts; data augmentation improves from 0% to 90%.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Systematic diagnosis of \\\\\\\"VLAs ignore language\\\\\\\" supports our story:\\\\n      instruction conditioning comes from input pathway, not training — even trained VLAs dont truly leverage language.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      Behavioral-level diagnosis + data augmentation solution. No representation analysis.\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison. No input pathway causal analysis.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2026-02-28. GitHub open-sourced.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # METHOD COMPONENT — RSA, CKA, Linear Probing\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: kriegeskorte2008\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Representational Similarity Analysis - Connecting the Branches of Systems Neuroscience\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Nikolaus Kriegeskorte, Marieke Mur, Peter Bandettini\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2008\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Frontiers in Systems Neuroscience\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: null\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"10.3389/neuro.06.004.2008\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Introduces Representational Dissimilarity Matrix (RDM) framework for comparing multi-channel\\\\n      neural activity measurements with computational models and behavior.\\\\n      Validated on fMRI visual cortex data.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: \\\\\\\"RSA is one of our core analysis methods for comparing trained/untrained/DINO representation structures.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Original RSA paper. 5000+ citations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kornblith2019\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Similarity of Neural Network Representations Revisited\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Simon Kornblith, Mohammad Norouzi, Honglak Lee, Geoffrey Hinton\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2019\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2019\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"1905.00414\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Introduces Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) as representation similarity metric.\\\\n      CKA reliably identifies representation correspondences across differently initialized networks,\\\\n      outperforming CCA-based methods.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      CKA is one of our core tools for comparing trained vs untrained representations.\\\\n      Deep-layer CKA reaching 0.86 demonstrates high representation similarity.\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Google Brain. CKA is now standard for representation comparison.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: alain2017\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Understanding Intermediate Layers Using Linear Classifier Probes\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Guillaume Alain, Yoshua Bengio\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2017\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2017 Workshop\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"1610.01644\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Proposes linear classifier probes to monitor feature suitability for classification at every layer.\\\\n      Linear separability increases monotonically along network depth.\\\\n      Validated on Inception v3 and ResNet-50.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: \\\\\\\"Linear probing is the methodological foundation for our goal classification and action prediction experiments.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Foundational probing methodology work.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # VISION ENCODERS — SigLIP, DINOv2, CLIP\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: zhai2023_siglip\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Sigmoid Loss for Language Image Pre-Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Xiaohua Zhai, Basil Mustafa, Alexander Kolesnikov, Lucas Beyer\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICCV 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2303.15343\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: vision-encoder\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Replaces softmax contrastive loss with pairwise sigmoid loss for VL pretraining.\\\\n      Eliminates global batch normalization, enabling smaller batch training.\\\\n      SigLIP-SO400M achieves competitive zero-shot performance with simpler training.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      SigLIP-SO400M is OpenVLA's frozen vision encoder. Our RSA=0.615 for SigLIP+Projector\\\\n      shows this encoder provides dominant action-relevant geometric structure.\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Google. OpenVLA uses SigLIP-SO400M/14 variant.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: oquab2024_dinov2\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DINOv2: Learning Robust Visual Features without Supervision\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Maxime Oquab, Timothée Darcet, Théo Moutakanni, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"TMLR 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2304.07193\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: vision-encoder\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Self-supervised ViT trained on curated 142M image dataset (LVD-142M).\\\\n      Produces general-purpose features competitive with task-specific models.\\\\n      No language supervision — purely vision self-supervised.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Vision-only baseline in our analysis. DINO RSA=0.388 << SigLIP 0.615,\\\\n      but DINO R²=0.712 ≈ Projector R²=0.706 — key geometric-functional dissociation.\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Meta AI. DINOv2 ViT-L/14 used as vision-only control.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: radford2021_clip\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Alec Radford, Jong Wook Kim, Chris Hallacy, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2021\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2021\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2103.00020\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: vision-encoder\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    verified: true\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Contrastive language-image pretraining on 400M image-text pairs.\\\\n      Zero-shot transfer competitive with supervised baselines.\\\\n      Pioneering VL pretraining approach.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      CLIP ViT-L/14 RSA=0.557 confirms VL pretraining universality:\\\\n      both CLIP (0.557) and SigLIP+Proj (0.615) >> DINO (0.388).\\\\n      Action-relevant geometric structure is a universal product of VL pretraining.\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"OpenAI. CLIP ViT-L/14 used as VL pretraining generalization control (plan_012).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # BACKGROUND — VLM Design, Frozen Encoders, Multi-modal Fusion\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: karamcheti2024\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Prismatic VLMs: Investigating the Design Space of Visually-Conditioned Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Siddharth Karamcheti, Suraj Nair, Ashwin Balakrishna, Percy Liang, Thomas Kollar, Dorsa Sadigh\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2402.07865\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Systematic investigation of VLM design space: pretrained visual representations, base vs instruct-tuned LM, etc.\\\\n      Different vision encoder ensembles (SigLIP, DINOv2) improve performance.\\\\n      OpenVLA is directly built on the Prismatic architecture.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      OpenVLA's VLM backbone origin. The SigLIP+DINOv2 fusion design choice\\\\n      is the architectural basis for our analysis of \\\\\\\"frozen vision encoder carries instruction conditioning.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      VLM design space exploration, no VLA-specific representation analysis.\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Significant author overlap with OpenVLA team.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: vla_survey2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) Models: Concepts, Progress, Applications and Challenges\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Multiple authors\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2505.04769\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Comprehensive survey of 100+ VLA architectures, 26 major VLA datasets.\\\\n      Large-scale quantitative analysis linking model design choices to normalized performance.\\\\n      Covers fusion strategy, action representation, encoder selection.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: \\\\\\\"Provides VLA landscape for positioning our work in the representation analysis sub-direction.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"Survey, no original experimental analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Most comprehensive VLA survey as of 2025-05.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # EVALUATION — LIBERO Benchmark Ecosystem\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: liu2023_libero\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LIBERO: Benchmarking Knowledge Transfer for Lifelong Robot Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Bo Liu, Yifeng Zhu, Chongkai Gao, Yihao Feng, Qiang Liu, Yuke Zhu, Peter Stone\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2306.03310\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      130 tasks across 4 suites (LIBERO-Spatial, LIBERO-Object, LIBERO-Goal, LIBERO-Long).\\\\n      Procedural generation + skill blending + human teleoperation demonstrations.\\\\n      Lifelong learning benchmark with knowledge transfer evaluation.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: \\\\\\\"Our primary experimental platform. LIBERO-10 (fine-tuning) + LIBERO-Goal (OOD test).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023 Datasets and Benchmarks track.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fei2025_libero_plus\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LIBERO-Plus: In-depth Robustness Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Senyu Fei, Siyin Wang, Junhao Shi, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2510.13626\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      7-dimension controlled perturbations expose VLA fragility.\\\\n      Performance drops from 95% to <30% (camera viewpoint, robot initial state most sensitive).\\\\n      Models are insensitive to language variations — largely ignore language instructions.\\\\n      Positional bias over genuine semantic understanding.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      \\\\\\\"Models ignore language instructions\\\\\\\" is consistent with our finding that\\\\n      training doesnt improve representation structure — VLAs have not truly learned language grounding.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"Behavioral robustness analysis, no representation-level analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-10. OpenMOSS team.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zhou2025_libero_pro\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Xueyang Zhou, Yangming Xu, Guiyao Tie, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2510.03827\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Standard LIBERO >90% accuracy, but generalized setting collapses to 0.0%.\\\\n      Exposes reliance on rote memorization (action sequences + environment layouts)\\\\n      rather than genuine task understanding or environmental perception.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      \\\\\\\"Memorization not understanding\\\\\\\" supports our story:\\\\n      VLA training improves action sequence memorization, not representation structure.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"Behavioral evaluation, no representation analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-10.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: libero_para2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LIBERO-Para: A Diagnostic Benchmark and Metrics for Paraphrase Robustness in VLA Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Multiple authors\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.28301\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      7 VLA configurations (0.6B-7.5B) show 22-52pp performance degradation under paraphrasing.\\\\n      80-96% of failures from planning-level trajectory divergence (not execution errors),\\\\n      indicating paraphrasing disrupts task identification.\\\\n      Synonym substitution alone causes large drops — relies on surface-level matching, not semantic grounding.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Further confirms VLAs lack true language grounding.\\\\n      Complements our \\\\\\\"instruction conditioning comes from input pathway structure, not semantic training.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"Paraphrase robustness diagnosis, no representation analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2026-03-30.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # RELATED ARCHITECTURE — VLA Models\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: kim2024_openvla\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Moo Jin Kim, Karl Pertsch, Siddharth Karamcheti, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2406.09246\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: related-architecture\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      7B-param VLA trained on 970k real-world robot demonstrations (Open X-Embodiment).\\\\n      Llama 2 7B backbone + DINOv2+SigLIP fused vision encoder.\\\\n      Outperforms RT-2-X (55B) by 16.5% absolute with 7x fewer parameters.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Our analysis target. All probing/RSA/CKA experiments use OpenVLA-7B.\\\\n      Architecture details (frozen SigLIP, Llama 2 backbone) directly affect our interpretation\\\\n      of \\\\\\\"input pathway vs training.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Stanford/Toyota/Google. Open-source checkpoints + training code.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: brohan2023_rt2\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"RT-2: Vision-Language-Action Models Transfer Web Knowledge to Robotic Control\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Anthony Brohan et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2307.15818\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: related-architecture\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Co-fine-tunes VLM (PaLM-E 55B) on robotic trajectory data + internet-scale VL tasks.\\\\n      Robot actions represented as text tokens.\\\\n      Demonstrates emergent capabilities: novel object generalization, chain-of-thought reasoning.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Pioneering VLA architecture. RT-2 uses PaLM-E, OpenVLA uses Llama 2,\\\\n      but shares the \\\\\\\"LLM backbone + frozen vision encoder\\\\\\\" core design.\\\\n      Our findings may generalize to similar architectures.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"No representation analysis. No trained vs untrained comparison.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Google DeepMind. 55B params, not open-source.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: ghosh2024_octo\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Robot Policy\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Octo Model Team, Dibya Ghosh, Homer Walke, Karl Pertsch, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2405.12213\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: related-architecture\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Transformer-based diffusion policy pretrained on 800k robot episodes (Open X-Embodiment).\\\\n      Lightweight CNN vision encoder + language model for text instructions.\\\\n      27M (Small) / 93M (Base) params, consumer GPU fine-tuning.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Alternative VLA architecture (not LLM-based). Grant 2026 tests include Octo.\\\\n      If our findings generalize to Octo, the input pathway effect is cross-architectural.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"No representation analysis. No frozen encoder analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Berkeley. Author overlap with OpenVLA.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: black2024_pi0\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"pi0: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Kevin Black et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2410.24164\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: related-architecture\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Flow matching action head (not autoregressive tokens), 50Hz continuous trajectories.\\\\n      Pre-trained VLM backbone + flow matching combination.\\\\n      7 robotic platforms, 68 tasks. Real-world dexterous manipulation.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      pi0 uses continuous flow matching vs discrete tokens — different action representation.\\\\n      Grant 2026 and Haon 2025 both tested Pi0/pi0.5, enabling cross-reference with our OpenVLA results.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"No representation structure analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Physical Intelligence. Commercial frontier VLA.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# CATEGORY INDEX\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\ncategories:\\\\n  direct-competitor:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"VLA representation / interpretability / language grounding analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - grant2026\\\\n      - swann2026\\\\n      - rizzardo2026\\\\n      - haon2025\\\\n      - molinari2025\\\\n      - lu2025\\\\n      - fang_intention2025\\\\n      - fang_cf2026\\\\n      - hou2026\\\\n\\\\n  method-component:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"RSA, CKA, linear probing methodological references\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - kriegeskorte2008\\\\n      - kornblith2019\\\\n      - alain2017\\\\n\\\\n  vision-encoder:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Vision encoder architectures used in our analysis (SigLIP, DINOv2, CLIP)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - zhai2023_siglip\\\\n      - oquab2024_dinov2\\\\n      - radford2021_clip\\\\n\\\\n  background:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"VLM design space, frozen encoder effects, multi-modal fusion\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - karamcheti2024\\\\n      - vla_survey2025\\\\n\\\\n  evaluation:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"LIBERO benchmark ecosystem and VLA robustness evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - liu2023_libero\\\\n      - fei2025_libero_plus\\\\n      - zhou2025_libero_pro\\\\n      - libero_para2026\\\\n\\\\n  related-architecture:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"VLA model architectures (OpenVLA, RT-2, Octo, pi0)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - kim2024_openvla\\\\n      - brohan2023_rt2\\\\n      - ghosh2024_octo\\\\n      - black2024_pi0\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# SYNTHESIS: OUR NOVELTY POSITION\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nsynthesis:\\\\n  our_core_claim: \\\\\\\"Instruction Input Pathway Drives Conditioning, Not Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  what_others_have_done:\\\\n    behavioral_level:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Grant 2026: vision pathway dominates, language 'encoded but not used' (activation injection)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LIBERO-CF/LangGap/LIBERO-Plus: VLAs ignore language instructions (counterfactual/robustness tests)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LIBERO-PRO: models rely on memorization not understanding\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Fang 2025: fine-tuning erodes VLM reasoning (intention != execution)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    representation_level:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Swann 2026: SAE features mostly memorized sequences, some interpretable (OpenVLA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Molinari 2025: world model emerges in middle layers (OpenVLA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Lu 2025: symbolic states decodable with >0.90 accuracy (OpenVLA + LIBERO)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Haon 2025: sparse semantic directions causally linked to actions (Pi0 + OpenVLA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Rizzardo 2026: feature-observability and controllability (pi0.5 + OpenVLA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  what_nobody_has_done:\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Trained vs untrained VLA representation comparison (we find trained ~= untrained >> DINO)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Instruction input pathway vs LLM training separation analysis (6 probing rounds + RSA all consistent)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Systematic layer-wise RSA/CKA comparison of trained/untrained/DINO representation structures\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Instruction ablation causal verification: removing instruction drops RSA to DINO level\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  key_differentiation_from_grant2026: >\\\\n    Grant 2026 compares different VLA models (architectural differences);\\\\n    we compare trained vs untrained within the same model (training effect isolation).\\\\n    Grant finds \\\\\\\"language encoded but not used\\\\\\\" (behavioral level);\\\\n    we find \\\\\\\"conditioning comes from input pathway, not training\\\\\\\" (representation level).\\\\n    Complementary, not competing. Related Work must qualify OFT vs standard fine-tuning difference.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 29243, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fkf1rVNGL1iMs2op5SNsMN","session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3","uuid":"db4181e7-7469-4d25-a7c6-2e35a887f196","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:23:04.092Z"}
