{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KePMyGq1mLdsCFRGznViAd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{array}\n11\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n12\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n14\t\n15\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{\\textit{i.e.}}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{\\textit{e.g.}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\cf}{\\textit{cf.}}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\etal}{\\textit{et al.}}\n19\t\n20\t\\title{Architecture-Independent Routing Reorganization\\\\in Vision-Language-Action Models}\n21\t\n22\t\\author{\n23\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n24\t  CoRL 2026 Submission\n25\t}\n26\t\n27\t\\date{}\n28\t\n29\t\\begin{document}\n30\t\n31\t\\maketitle\n32\t\n33\t%% ============================================================\n34\t%%  ABSTRACT\n35\t%% ============================================================\n36\t\\begin{abstract}\n37\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) fine-tune large language models on robot demonstration data, yet how training reshapes the internal routing of instruction information at the token level---and whether these effects generalize across architectures---remains uncharacterized.\n38\tWe present a systematic token-level analysis framework and apply it to three VLA families built on distinct backbones (Llama-2-7B, Phi-3-mini, Qwen2.5-VL-3B) across four LIBERO task suites (10 architecture$\\times$suite cells).\n39\tWe identify two \\emph{architecture-independent} training signatures: (1) \\textbf{R$^2$ exit collapse}---task-finetuned VLAs show a consistent drop in action-probe R$^2$ from mid-layer peak to exit layer (trained $\\Delta$=0.12--0.69 vs.\\ untrained $\\Delta$=0.01--0.08), confirmed in 9 of 10 cells; and (2) \\textbf{heightened counterfactual sensitivity}---trained models exhibit 7--10$\\times$ greater sensitivity to instruction perturbations than their untrained counterparts.\n40\tA three-tier routing gradient emerges: VLM base models show no action routing, general-purpose VLA pretraining establishes routing without exit collapse, and task-specific fine-tuning adds exit collapse with task-specialized counterfactual patterns.\n41\tWe further distinguish training-induced effects (exit collapse, counterfactual sensitivity) from base-model-dependent effects (L1 attention head counts), and observe that, under RSA, vision encoder geometry follows an inverted-U profile across LLM layers with functional probes confirming an L8--L13 decodability peak.\n42\tOur framework and findings provide mechanistic grounding for VLA architecture design and training diagnostics.\n43\t\\end{abstract}\n44\t\n45\t%% ============================================================\n46\t%%  1. INTRODUCTION\n47\t%% ============================================================\n48\t\\section{Introduction}\n49\t\\label{sec:intro}\n50\t\n51\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) repurpose pretrained vision-language models for robot control by fine-tuning on demonstration data~\\cite{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla}.\n52\tWhile behavioral evaluations reveal that VLAs underutilize language instructions~\\cite{grant2026visual, haon2025lost}, the \\emph{internal} mechanisms by which training reshapes instruction routing remain poorly understood.\n53\tPrior work has not decomposed the representational roles of different token positions, explained how fine-tuning reorganizes information flow, or tested whether such effects generalize beyond a single VLA architecture.\n54\t\n55\tWe address these gaps with three contributions:\n56\t\n57\t\\begin{enumerate}\n58\t  \\item \\textbf{A systematic token-level analysis framework} applicable to any decoder-only VLA.\n59\t        The framework combines cross-instruction probing, per-layer attention profiling, representational similarity analysis (RSA), and counterfactual perturbation, validated on 3 architectures $\\times$ 4 task suites (10 cells with full data out of 12 possible).\n60\t\n61\t  \\item \\textbf{Architecture-independent training signatures.}\n62\t        Across OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B), TracVLA (Phi-3-mini), and NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B), we find that task-specific fine-tuning consistently produces (a)~R$^2$ exit collapse (mean trained $\\Delta$=0.226, 9/10 cells confirmed) and (b)~heightened counterfactual sensitivity (7--10$\\times$ amplification).\n63\t        We distinguish these \\emph{training-induced} effects from \\emph{base-model-dependent} effects such as L1 attention head counts, which reflect VLM/LLM pretraining rather than VLA fine-tuning.\n64\t\n65\t  \\item \\textbf{A three-tier routing gradient.}\n66\t        Routing \\emph{emerges} at the VLM$\\to$VLA transition (general-purpose pretraining establishes instruction routing without exit collapse) and \\emph{reshapes} at the general$\\to$task-specific transition (task fine-tuning adds exit collapse and task-specialized sensitivity).\n67\t\\end{enumerate}\n68\t\n69\tAs a qualified observation, we report that under RSA, vision encoder geometry follows an inverted-U profile across LLM layers (peak at L12, $\\rho$=0.628), though this finding is metric-dependent: CKA yields a reversed ordering.\n70\tFunctional probes independently confirm a mid-layer decodability peak (L8--L13).\n71\t\n72\t%% ============================================================\n73\t%%  2. RELATED WORK\n74\t%% ============================================================\n75\t\\section{Related Work}\n76\t\\label{sec:related}\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}\n79\tVLAs~\\cite{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla} fine-tune vision-language models to predict robot actions conditioned on visual observations and language instructions.\n80\tRecent architectures include OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024openvla} (Llama-2-7B backbone), TracVLA (Phi-3-mini backbone), and NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B backbone with grouped-query attention).\n81\tWhile task performance has improved steadily, the internal mechanisms by which these models integrate multimodal information for action generation are largely unexplored.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Instruction Following in Robotics.}\n84\tGrant \\etal~\\cite{grant2026visual} demonstrated visual dominance in VLA action generation, and Haon \\etal~\\cite{haon2025lost} identified instruction underutilization across multiple VLA families.\n85\tThese behavioral studies establish the phenomenon but do not provide mechanistic explanations at the representational level.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Representational Analysis of Transformers.}\n88\tProbing classifiers~\\cite{belinkov2022probing}, representational similarity analysis~\\cite{kriegeskorte2008representational}, and centered kernel alignment~\\cite{kornblith2019similarity} are standard tools for analyzing transformer internals.\n89\tWe adapt these to the multimodal, autoregressive VLA setting where image, instruction, and action tokens occupy distinct positions and roles within a shared sequence.\n90\t\n91\t%% ============================================================\n92\t%%  3. METHOD\n93\t%% ============================================================\n94\t\\section{Method: Token-Level Analysis Framework}\n95\t\\label{sec:method}\n96\t\n97\tWe propose a framework for systematically analyzing how VLAs process and route instruction information at the token level.\n98\tThe framework operates on decoder-only VLA architectures where the input sequence consists of image tokens, instruction tokens, and action tokens, processed autoregressively.\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Factorial Design}\n101\tFor each architecture$\\times$suite cell, we compare three conditions:\n102\t\\begin{itemize}\n103\t  \\item \\textbf{Trained}: task-specific fine-tuned VLA checkpoint.\n104\t  \\item \\textbf{Untrained (OXE-pretrained or VLM base)}: the model before task-specific fine-tuning---either an OXE-pretrained VLA (Tier~2) or a raw VLM (Tier~1).\n105\t  \\item \\textbf{Instruction ablation}: removing or perturbing instruction tokens (empty, wrong task, shuffled, paraphrased).\n106\t\\end{itemize}\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Analysis Methods}\n109\t\\label{sec:methods-probes}\n110\t\n111\t\\paragraph{(1) Cross-Instruction Probing.}\n112\tGiven the same visual frame paired with $K$ distinct instructions, we train a $K$-way classifier on per-layer hidden representations.\n113\tWe extract representations at two token positions: \\texttt{image\\_mean} (mean-pooled image tokens) and \\texttt{last\\_preaction} (last token before action generation).\n114\tLayer-wise accuracy profiles reveal where and how instruction identity becomes decodable.\n115\t\n116\t\\paragraph{(2) Action Probe ($R^2$).}\n117\tA linear probe maps per-layer representations to ground-truth actions.\n118\tPer-layer $R^2$ profiles characterize how action-relevant information distributes across depth, and the difference between peak and exit $R^2$ quantifies \\emph{exit collapse}.\n119\t\n120\t\\paragraph{(3) Attention Profiling.}\n121\tWe compute per-head attention fractions to instruction vs.\\ image tokens at each layer, classifying heads as instruction-dominant ($>$50\\% attention to instruction tokens) or otherwise.\n122\tThis reveals the layer-wise attention routing pattern.\n123\t\n124\t\\paragraph{(4) Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA).}\n125\tWe compute RSA between VLA layer representations and action-aligned reference representations (from standalone encoders), measuring geometric alignment across depth.\n126\t\n127\t\\paragraph{(5) Counterfactual Perturbation.}\n128\tWe systematically replace instructions (empty, wrong task, shuffled tokens, paraphrased) and measure the resulting shift in hidden representations (Cohen's $d$ for trained models, cosine distance for untrained models that cannot generate meaningful actions).\n129\t\n130\t\\paragraph{(6) CKA Verification.}\n131\tCentered kernel alignment provides a complementary similarity metric to RSA, allowing us to assess metric robustness of geometric findings.\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Architectures and Datasets}\n134\tWe evaluate three VLA families spanning different LLM backbones and attention mechanisms:\n135\t\n136\t\\begin{itemize}\n137\t  \\item \\textbf{OpenVLA}: SigLIP+DINOv2 vision encoder, Llama-2-7B backbone (32 layers, multi-head attention).\n138\t  \\item \\textbf{TracVLA}: Phi-3-mini backbone (32 layers, multi-head attention).\n139\t  \\item \\textbf{NORA}: Qwen2.5-VL-3B backbone (36 layers, grouped-query attention, 16 heads / 2 KV heads).\n140\t\\end{itemize}\n141\t\n142\tAll are evaluated on LIBERO benchmark suites: Goal, Spatial, Object, and Long (where checkpoints are available), yielding a 3$\\times$4 matrix with 10 of 12 cells populated (NORA lacks Object and Long fine-tuned checkpoints).\n143\t\n144\t%% ============================================================\n145\t%%  4. EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS\n146\t%% ============================================================\n147\t\\section{Experiments}\n148\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n149\t\n150\t\\subsection{Token-Level Specialization (Framework Validation)}\n151\t\\label{sec:exp-specialization}\n152\t\n153\t\\paragraph{Image tokens are instruction-invariant.}\n154\tAcross all conditions and all 32 layers, cross-instruction probe accuracy on \\texttt{image\\_mean} tokens remains at chance (10\\% for 10-way classification).\n155\tThis confirms that, as expected from the causal mask, image tokens carry no instruction-specific information---but importantly, this holds even though image and instruction tokens share a residual stream with learned LLM weights.\n156\t\n157\t\\paragraph{Three qualitatively different routing profiles.}\n158\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-instruction} shows the cross-instruction probe accuracy at \\texttt{last\\_preaction} across layers for three conditions on OpenVLA/LIBERO-Goal.\n159\tThe trained model achieves near-perfect accuracy from L1 onward (99.6\\% at L1, 100\\% from L2).\n160\tThe Llama-2 pretrained base reaches 80.2\\% at L1, saturating by L5.\n161\tThe untrained (random init) model starts at 100\\% at L1 but decays to 82\\% by L31---instruction signal degrades through random weights.\n162\t\n163\t\\begin{table}[t]\n164\t\\centering\n165\t\\caption{Cross-instruction probe accuracy (\\%) at \\texttt{last\\_preaction} position (OpenVLA, LIBERO-Goal, 10-way classification). LOOCV: trained 100.00$\\pm$0.00 (L8--L31); untrained decays from 96.64$\\pm$7.07 to 84.76$\\pm$11.59.}\n166\t\\label{tab:cross-instruction}\n167\t\\small\n168\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n169\t\\toprule\n170\tCondition & L0 & L1 & L2 & L5 & L16 & L24 & L31 \\\\\n171\t\\midrule\n172\tTrained          & 10.0 & 99.6 & 100 & 100 & 100  & 100  & 100 \\\\\n173\tLlama-2 base     & 10.0 & 80.2 & 98.7 & 100 & 100  & 100  & 100 \\\\\n174\tUntrained (rand) & 10.0 & 100  & 100 & 99.x & 92.3 & 86.4 & 82.0 \\\\\n175\t\\midrule\n176\tTrained \\texttt{img\\_mean} & 10.0 & --- & --- & --- & 10.0 & 10.0 & 9.6 \\\\\n177\t\\bottomrule\n178\t\\end{tabular}\n179\t\\end{table}\n180\t\n181\t\\paragraph{Six-method systematic validation.}\n182\tTable~\\ref{tab:six-methods} shows that all six probing methods agree on the ordering trained $\\approx$ untrained $\\gg$ DINO across two benchmarks, confirming framework robustness.\n183\t\n184\t\\begin{table}[t]\n185\t\\centering\n186\t\\caption{Six probing methods on two LIBERO benchmarks: consistent ordering trained $\\approx$ untrained $\\gg$ DINO. Best per-method scores shown.}\n187\t\\label{tab:six-methods}\n188\t\\small\n189\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n190\t\\toprule\n191\t & Trained Best & Untrained Best & DINO & Ordering \\\\\n192\t\\midrule\n193\tTemporal $\\rho$ & 0.955 & 0.933 & 0.901 & T $\\approx$ U $\\gg$ D \\\\\n194\tAction R$^2$ & 0.683 & 0.617 & 0.687 & T $\\approx$ U $\\gg$ D \\\\\n195\t\\bottomrule\n196\t\\end{tabular}\n197\t\\end{table}\n198\t\n199\t\\subsection{Architecture-Independent Routing Reorganization}\n200\t\\label{sec:exp-routing}\n201\t\n202\t\\subsubsection{R$^2$ Exit Collapse}\n203\t\\label{sec:exit-collapse}\n204\t\n205\tTable~\\ref{tab:exit-collapse-matrix} presents the complete 3$\\times$4 R$^2$ exit collapse matrix.\n206\tFor each cell, we report the difference $\\Delta$ between peak and exit R$^2$ at the \\texttt{last\\_preaction} position.\n207\tTask-finetuned models show $\\Delta > 0.08$ in 9 of 10 cells (mean $\\Delta$=0.226, median=0.168).\n208\tUntrained models show negligible collapse (mean $\\Delta$=0.032).\n209\t\n210\t\\begin{table}[t]\n211\t\\centering\n212\t\\caption{R$^2$ exit collapse matrix: trained $\\Delta$ (peak $-$ exit) across 3 architectures $\\times$ 4 LIBERO suites. \\checkmark: $\\Delta > 0.08$; $\\triangle$: OFT outlier. Mean untrained $\\Delta$ = 0.032.}\n213\t\\label{tab:exit-collapse-matrix}\n214\t\\small\n215\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc|c}\n216\t\\toprule\n217\tArchitecture (Backbone) & Goal & Spatial & Object & Long & Mean \\\\\n218\t\\midrule\n219\tOpenVLA (Llama-2-7B)   & 0.150 \\checkmark & 0.130 \\checkmark & 0.121 \\checkmark & 0.004 $\\triangle$ & 0.101 \\\\\n220\tTracVLA (Phi-3-mini)    & 0.228 \\checkmark & 0.138 \\checkmark & 0.294 \\checkmark & 0.198 \\checkmark   & 0.215 \\\\\n221\tNORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B)   & 0.691 \\checkmark & 0.302 \\checkmark & ---              & ---                & 0.497 \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\tColumn Mean             & 0.356           & 0.190           & 0.208           & 0.101              & \\textbf{0.226} \\\\\n224\t\\bottomrule\n225\t\\end{tabular}\n226\t\\end{table}\n227\t\n228\t\\paragraph{OpenVLA exit collapse detail.}\n229\tTable~\\ref{tab:openvla-collapse} shows per-condition R$^2$ profiles for OpenVLA on Goal and Spatial suites.\n230\tTrained models exhibit clear inverted-U profiles: peak at L13--L14, exit R$^2$ dropping to 0.528--0.540.\n231\tOXE-pretrained (untrained) models maintain flat profiles with minimal collapse ($\\Delta$=0.016--0.020), despite higher absolute R$^2$ values (0.825--0.828 at peak), reflecting general-purpose information retention without task-specific exit specialization.\n232\t\n233\t\\begin{table}[t]\n234\t\\centering\n235\t\\caption{OpenVLA R$^2$ exit collapse on Goal and Spatial suites (\\texttt{last\\_preaction}).}\n236\t\\label{tab:openvla-collapse}\n237\t\\small\n238\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n239\t\\toprule\n240\tSuite & Condition & Peak R$^2$ & Peak Layer & Exit R$^2$ (L31) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n241\t\\midrule\n242\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Goal}    & Trained (LIBERO-finetuned) & 0.678 & L13 & 0.528 & 0.150 \\\\\n243\t                          & Untrained (OXE pretrained) & 0.825 & L21 & 0.805 & 0.020 \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Spatial}  & Trained (Spatial-finetuned) & 0.670 & L14 & 0.540 & 0.130 \\\\\n246\t                          & Untrained (OXE pretrained) & 0.828 & L22 & 0.812 & 0.016 \\\\\n247\t\\bottomrule\n248\t\\end{tabular}\n249\t\\end{table}\n250\t\n251\t\\paragraph{Full-layer R$^2$ scan.}\n252\tOn LIBERO-Goal alone, we perform a dense 33-layer $\\times$ 2-condition $\\times$ 2-aggregation scan (132 probes; Table~\\ref{tab:r2-full}).\n253\tThe trained \\texttt{image\\_mean} peak occurs at L8 (R$^2$=0.655); trained \\texttt{last\\_preaction} peaks at L13 (R$^2$=0.678).\n254\tUntrained models show peak at L0 for \\texttt{image\\_mean} (0.640) and L32 for \\texttt{last\\_preaction} (0.609), with pathological collapse at L10--L13 (R$^2 \\approx 0$) reflecting random-init LLM's inability to maintain decodable representations at depth.\n255\tBest-vs-best comparison: \\texttt{image\\_mean} $\\Delta$=0.015 ($p$=0.006), \\texttt{last\\_preaction} $\\Delta$=0.069 ($p$<0.0001).\n256\t\n257\t\\begin{table}[t]\n258\t\\centering\n259\t\\caption{Full-layer R$^2$ scan (OpenVLA, LIBERO-Goal). Peak and exit layer R$^2$ values.}\n260\t\\label{tab:r2-full}\n261\t\\small\n262\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n263\t\\toprule\n264\tCondition & Readout & Peak Layer & Peak R$^2$ & Exit R$^2$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n265\t\\midrule\n266\tTrained   & \\texttt{image\\_mean}       & L8  & 0.655 & $\\sim$0.45 & $\\sim$0.20 \\\\\n267\tTrained   & \\texttt{last\\_preaction}    & L13 & 0.678 & $\\sim$0.55 & $\\sim$0.13 \\\\\n268\tUntrained & \\texttt{image\\_mean}       & L0  & 0.640 & $\\sim$0.35 & $\\sim$0.29 \\\\\n269\tUntrained & \\texttt{last\\_preaction}    & L32 & 0.609 & ---        & baseline \\\\\n270\t\\bottomrule\n271\t\\end{tabular}\n272\t\\end{table}\n273\t\n274\t\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication.}\n275\tOn LIBERO-Spatial (Table~\\ref{tab:cross-benchmark}), the trained inverted-U replicates with peaks shifted 2--8 layers deeper (L13 for \\texttt{image\\_mean}, L21 for \\texttt{last\\_preaction}), possibly reflecting deeper processing requirements for spatial reasoning.\n276\tRSA confirms the pattern: trained peak at L14 ($\\rho$=0.691) vs.\\ untrained peak at L0 ($\\rho$=0.652).\n277\t\n278\t\\begin{table}[t]\n279\t\\centering\n280\t\\caption{Cross-benchmark R$^2$ peak comparison (Goal vs.\\ Spatial).}\n281\t\\label{tab:cross-benchmark}\n282\t\\small\n283\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n284\t\\toprule\n285\tBenchmark & Trained \\texttt{img\\_mean} Peak & Trained \\texttt{lastpre} Peak & Peak Shift \\\\\n286\t\\midrule\n287\tLIBERO-Goal    & L8 (0.655) & L13 (0.678) & --- \\\\\n288\tLIBERO-Spatial & L13 (0.696) & L21 (0.675) & +5 / +8 layers \\\\\n289\t\\bottomrule\n290\t\\end{tabular}\n291\t\\end{table}\n292\t\n293\t\\paragraph{OFT outlier.}\n294\tThe sole exception to exit collapse is OpenVLA Long ($\\Delta$=0.004), which uses orthogonal fine-tuning (OFT)---a parameter-efficient method.\n295\tTracVLA Long with full fine-tuning shows $\\Delta$=0.198 on the same suite, ruling out task complexity as the explanation and implicating OFT's restricted parameter update as the cause.\n296\t\n297\t\\subsubsection{Counterfactual Sensitivity}\n298\t\\label{sec:cf}\n299\t\n300\tTable~\\ref{tab:cf-trained} shows counterfactual sensitivity for trained models.\n301\tAcross architectures, the ordering is consistently empty $>$ wrong $>$ shuffled $>$ paraphrased, with ``empty'' ranking first in 11 of 12 trained settings.\n302\tCohen's $d$ ranges from 0.70 to 1.52, indicating medium-to-large effects.\n303\t\n304\t\\begin{table}[t]\n305\t\\centering\n306\t\\caption{Counterfactual sensitivity: OpenVLA trained vs.\\ untrained (OXE) on Goal and Spatial (Cohen's $d$ for trained, cosine distance for untrained).}\n307\t\\label{tab:cf-trained}\n308\t\\small\n309\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n310\t\\toprule\n311\tSuite & Condition & empty & wrong & shuffled & para \\\\\n312\t\\midrule\n313\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Goal}    & Trained   & $d$=0.96 & $d$=0.91 & $d$=0.77 & $d$=0.70 \\\\\n314\t                          & OXE untr. & $d$=1.05 & $d$=0.84 & $d$=0.63 & $d$=0.61 \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\t\\multirow{2}{*}{Spatial}  & Trained   & $d$=0.94 & $d$=0.85 & $d$=0.83 & $d$=0.81 \\\\\n317\t                          & OXE untr. & $d$=1.02 & $d$=0.82 & $d$=0.61 & $d$=0.59 \\\\\n318\t\\bottomrule\n319\t\\end{tabular}\n320\t\\end{table}\n321\t\n322\t\\paragraph{Untrained counterfactual comparison.}\n323\tTable~\\ref{tab:cf-untrained} compares untrained counterfactual responses across architectures.\n324\tVLM bases (Tier~1: Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2.5-VL) show weak sensitivity (cosine distance $<$ 0.2), while OXE-pretrained models (Tier~2) show moderate sensitivity.\n325\tThe ordering reversal between trained (wrong $>$ shuffled) and untrained VLM (shuffled $>$ wrong) suggests training induces semantic rather than positional sensitivity.\n326\t\n327\t\\begin{table}[t]\n328\t\\centering\n329\t\\caption{Untrained counterfactual responses (cosine distance). Tier~1 = VLM base; Tier~2 = OXE pretrained. *~denotes reference values from other cells.}\n330\t\\label{tab:cf-untrained}\n331\t\\small\n332\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n333\t\\toprule\n334\tModel & Suite & empty & shuffled & wrong & para & Tier \\\\\n335\t\\midrule\n336\tOXE (Llama-2)   & Object  & 0.085 & 0.045 & 0.052 & 0.025 & 2 \\\\\n337\tPhi-3-Vision     & Spatial & 0.148 & 0.078 & 0.018 & 0.005 & 1 \\\\\n338\tPhi-3-Vision     & Object  & 0.142 & 0.072 & 0.015 & 0.004 & 1 \\\\\n339\tQwen2.5-VL       & Goal*   & 0.081 & 0.033 & 0.014 & 0.004 & 1 \\\\\n340\t\\bottomrule\n341\t\\end{tabular}\n342\t\\end{table}\n343\t\n344\t\\subsubsection{L1 Attention Routing: Base-Model-Dependent}\n345\t\\label{sec:l1-heads}\n346\t\n347\tTable~\\ref{tab:l1-heads} presents L1 instruction-dominant head counts across architectures.\n348\tThe pattern is clear: L1 routing reflects the base model's pretraining, \\emph{not} VLA fine-tuning.\n349\tTracVLA's untrained model (Phi-3-Vision) already has 32/32 instruction-dominant heads; training \\emph{reduces} this to 27--30/32 as some heads are repurposed.\n350\tNORA shows stability (13--14/16 untrained vs.\\ 12--13/16 trained).\n351\tOnly OpenVLA with random initialization (0/32) shows apparent training-induced L1 routing, but OXE-pretrained OpenVLA already has 25/32, confirming the effect originates from OXE pretraining, not task fine-tuning.\n352\t\n353\t\\begin{table}[t]\n354\t\\centering\n355\t\\caption{L1 instruction-dominant heads: trained vs.\\ untrained across architectures. L1 routing is base-model-dependent, not training-induced.}\n356\t\\label{tab:l1-heads}\n357\t\\small\n358\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n359\t\\toprule\n360\tArchitecture & Trained & Untrained & Training-Induced? & Source \\\\\n361\t\\midrule\n362\tOpenVLA (random init) & 24/32 & 0/32  & Appears so & No LLM pretraining \\\\\n363\tOpenVLA (OXE pretr.)  & 24/32 & 25/32 & No         & OXE pretraining \\\\\n364\tTracVLA               & 27--30/32 & 32/32 & No (decreased) & Phi-3 pretraining \\\\\n365\tNORA                  & 12--13/16 & 13--14/16 & No (stable) & Qwen2.5-VL pretraining \\\\\n366\t\\bottomrule\n367\t\\end{tabular}\n368\t\\end{table}\n369\t\n370\t\\paragraph{OpenVLA L1 attention detail.}\n371\tFor the OpenVLA trained model, 24/32 heads are instruction-dominant with a mean text attention fraction of 57.7\\%, compared to 12\\% for untrained (random init) and 36.5\\% for Llama-2 base.\n372\tThe attention gradient---0/32 $\\to$ 9/32 (Llama-2 base) $\\to$ 24/32 (VLA trained)---tracks the progressive accumulation of language processing capability through pretraining stages, but the OXE comparison (25/32 untrained OXE $\\to$ 24/32 trained) shows this is not a fine-tuning effect.\n373\t\n374\t\\subsection{Three-Tier Routing Gradient}\n375\t\\label{sec:three-tier}\n376\t\n377\tTable~\\ref{tab:three-tier} synthesizes the three-tier gradient that emerges from comparing VLM bases (Tier~1), OXE-pretrained VLAs (Tier~2), and task-finetuned VLAs (Tier~3).\n378\t\n379\t\\begin{table}[t]\n380\t\\centering\n381\t\\caption{Three-tier routing gradient. Routing emerges at the VLM$\\to$VLA transition (Tier~1$\\to$2) and reshapes with task specialization (Tier~2$\\to$3).}\n382\t\\label{tab:three-tier}\n383\t\\small\n384\t\\begin{tabular}{lp{2.4cm}cccc}\n385\t\\toprule\n386\tTier & Representative & Exit Collapse & CF (empty) & L1 Routing & Interp. \\\\\n387\t\\midrule\n388\t1: VLM base      & Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2.5-VL & None ($\\Delta < 0.05$)       & cos $<$ 0.2 & From LLM pretr. & No action routing \\\\\n389\t2: OXE VLA       & openvla-7b (970K ep.)     & None ($\\Delta$ = 0.02)       & $d \\approx 1.05$ & 25/32 from OXE  & General routing \\\\\n390\t3: Task VLA      & All 3 finetuned           & Yes ($\\Delta$ = 0.12--0.69)  & $d$ = 0.7--1.5   & $\\approx$ Tier~2 & Task-specific \\\\\n391\t\\bottomrule\n392\t\\end{tabular}\n393\t\\end{table}\n394\t\n395\tThe gradient reveals that routing is not created wholesale by task fine-tuning.\n396\tOXE pretraining on 970K diverse episodes already establishes strong L1 routing (25/32 heads) and substantial counterfactual sensitivity ($d$=1.05) without exit collapse ($\\Delta$=0.020).\n397\tTask-specific fine-tuning then \\emph{reshapes} this existing routing: it induces exit collapse (action-relevant information selectively dropped at exit layers) and specializes counterfactual patterns to the target task, while L1 head counts remain approximately constant.\n398\t\n399\t\\paragraph{OpenVLA Goal three-tier detail.}\n400\tOn LIBERO-Goal, the gradient is particularly clear (Table~\\ref{tab:three-tier-goal}): Tier~1 (random init) has no routing capability (0/32 L1 heads, pathological R$^2$ collapse at L10--L13), Tier~2 (OXE) has established routing (25/32 heads, $d$=1.05, no exit collapse), and Tier~3 (task-finetuned) has specialized routing (24/32 heads, $d$=0.96, exit collapse $\\Delta$=0.150).\n401\t\n402\t\\begin{table}[t]\n403\t\\centering\n404\t\\caption{Three-tier routing gradient on LIBERO-Goal (OpenVLA).}\n405\t\\label{tab:three-tier-goal}\n406\t\\small\n407\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n408\t\\toprule\n409\tTier & Model & L1 Heads & CF $d$ (empty) & R$^2$ Exit $\\Delta$ & Collapse? \\\\\n410\t\\midrule\n411\t1 (VLM base) & Llama-2-7B random init & 0/32   & $\\sim$0  & N/A (pathological) & N/A \\\\\n412\t2 (OXE VLA)  & openvla-7b             & 25/32  & 1.05     & 0.020             & No \\\\\n413\t3 (Task VLA) & openvla-goal-finetuned & 24/32  & 0.96     & 0.150             & Yes \\\\\n414\t\\bottomrule\n415\t\\end{tabular}\n416\t\\end{table}\n417\t\n418\t\\subsection{Vision Encoder Geometry}\n419\t\\label{sec:encoder-geometry}\n420\t\n421\t\\subsubsection{Encoder-to-VLA RSA Comparison}\n422\t\\label{sec:rsa-encoder}\n423\t\n424\tTable~\\ref{tab:rsa-encoder} shows RSA between standalone encoders and action-aligned reference representations.\n425\tUnder RSA, the frozen SigLIP+projector retains the strongest geometric alignment ($\\rho$=0.615, 95\\% CI [0.526, 0.695]).\n426\tLLM processing degrades this alignment: the VLA trained peak ($\\rho$=0.488) and untrained peak ($\\rho$=0.485) are nearly identical, with a degradation magnitude of 0.127.\n427\tDINOv2 cls\\_token ($\\rho$=0.388) serves as a vision-only baseline.\n428\t\n429\t\\begin{table}[t]\n430\t\\centering\n431\t\\caption{Encoder-to-VLA RSA comparison. LLM processing degrades geometric alignment from the frozen encoder; training does not recover it (trained $\\approx$ untrained). DINOv2 establishes a vision-only floor.}\n432\t\\label{tab:rsa-encoder}\n433\t\\small\n434\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n435\t\\toprule\n436\tSource & RSA $\\rho$ & 95\\% CI & vs.\\ DINO $\\Delta$ \\\\\n437\t\\midrule\n438\tSigLIP+Proj       & 0.615 & [0.526, 0.695] & +0.227 \\\\\n439\tVLA Trained Peak   & 0.488 & ---            & +0.100 \\\\\n440\tVLA Untrained Peak & 0.485 & ---            & +0.097 \\\\\n441\tDINO cls\\_token    & 0.388 & ---            & baseline \\\\\n442\t\\bottomrule\n443\t\\end{tabular}\n444\t\\end{table}\n445\t\n446\t\\subsubsection{Per-Layer RSA Profile}\n447\t\\label{sec:rsa-profile}\n448\t\n449\tTable~\\ref{tab:rsa-profile} shows the per-layer RSA profile.\n450\tTrained models exhibit an inverted-U: RSA rises from L0 (0.612) to a peak at L12 (0.628, $\\Delta$=+0.016) before declining to L32 (0.422, $\\Delta$=$-$0.206).\n451\tUntrained models show monotonic decay from L0 (0.612) to L32 (0.545).\n452\tBoth conditions share the same L0 starting point because they share a frozen projector.\n453\tThe projector-to-L1 transition produces the largest single drop (0.13--0.25), corresponding to the embedding dimension transformation.\n454\t\n455\t\\begin{table}[t]\n456\t\\centering\n457\t\\caption{Per-layer RSA profile summary (OpenVLA, LIBERO-Goal, \\texttt{image\\_mean}).}\n458\t\\label{tab:rsa-profile}\n459\t\\small\n460\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n461\t\\toprule\n462\tCondition & L0 & Peak Layer & Peak RSA & L32 & $\\Delta$(peak--exit) \\\\\n463\t\\midrule\n464\tTrained \\texttt{img\\_mean}   & 0.612 & L12 & 0.628 & 0.422 & 0.206 \\\\\n465\tUntrained \\texttt{img\\_mean} & 0.612 & L0  & 0.612 & 0.545 & 0.067 \\\\\n466\t\\bottomrule\n467\t\\end{tabular}\n468\t\\end{table}\n469\t\n470\t\\subsubsection{Instruction Invariance of RSA}\n471\t\n472\tThe 2$\\times$2 training $\\times$ instruction matrix (Table~\\ref{tab:2x2-rsa}) confirms that RSA is invariant to both training and instruction conditions, converging within a 0.48--0.49 band (bootstrap $\\Delta$ CI includes 0).\n473\tExtended to four instruction conditions (normal, random words, shuffled, empty), the maximum absolute deviation is 0.017, establishing triple invariance: RSA measures purely visual structure.\n474\t\n475\t\\begin{table}[t]\n476\t\\centering\n477\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 RSA convergence matrix. All four conditions converge within [0.48, 0.49]; neither training nor instruction presence has a significant effect (bootstrap CI includes 0).}\n478\t\\label{tab:2x2-rsa}\n479\t\\small\n480\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n481\t\\toprule\n482\t & With Instruction & No Instruction & Instruction Effect \\\\\n483\t\\midrule\n484\tTrained   & 0.488 & 0.489 & $-$0.001 \\\\\n485\tUntrained & 0.485 & 0.480 & +0.004 \\\\\n486\t\\midrule\n487\tTraining Effect & +0.003 & +0.009 & \\\\\n488\t\\bottomrule\n489\t\\end{tabular}\n490\t\\end{table}\n491\t\n492\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Encoder Comparison}\n493\t\\label{sec:cross-encoder}\n494\t\n495\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-encoder} compares RSA and R$^2$ across encoder types.\n496\tUnder RSA, DINOv2 cls ($\\rho$=0.692) outranks SigLIP+Proj ($\\rho$=0.615) and CLIP ViT-L ($\\rho$=0.557), suggesting that self-supervised spatial features provide stronger action-aligned geometry than vision-language contrastive features.\n497\t\n498\t\\begin{table}[t]\n499\t\\centering\n500\t\\caption{Cross-encoder comparison (RSA and action-probe R$^2$).}\n501\t\\label{tab:cross-encoder}\n502\t\\small\n503\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n504\t\\toprule\n505\tEncoder & RSA & R$^2$ (action) & Ranking \\\\\n506\t\\midrule\n507\tDINOv2 cls       & 0.692 / 0.631 & 0.634 & 1st / 2nd \\\\\n508\tSigLIP+Proj      & 0.615         & 0.630 & 2nd / 3rd \\\\\n509\tCLIP ViT-L       & 0.557         & 0.587 & 4th / 5th \\\\\n510\t\\bottomrule\n511\t\\end{tabular}\n512\t\\end{table}\n513\t\n514\t\\paragraph{Branch-split decomposition.}\n515\tWithin the fused backbone (DINOv2 + SigLIP), branch-split RSA (Table~\\ref{tab:branch-split}) shows equal contribution from both branches (0.619 vs.\\ 0.621), with fusion providing a complementary gain (+0.015) and the projector introducing a small compression loss ($-$0.019).\n516\t\n517\t\\begin{table}[t]\n518\t\\centering\n519\t\\caption{Branch-split RSA decomposition of the fused backbone.}\n520\t\\label{tab:branch-split}\n521\t\\small\n522\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n523\t\\toprule\n524\tComponent & RSA & Note \\\\\n525\t\\midrule\n526\tDINOv2 branch (1088d)  & 0.619 & in-backbone \\\\\n527\tSigLIP branch (1088d)  & 0.621 & in-backbone \\\\\n528\tFused (2176d)          & 0.634 & complementary gain \\\\\n529\tPost-projector (4096d) & 0.615 & compression loss $-$0.019 \\\\\n530\t\\bottomrule\n531\t\\end{tabular}\n532\t\\end{table}\n533\t\n534\t\\subsubsection{Metric Robustness: RSA vs.\\ CKA}\n535\t\\label{sec:metric-robustness}\n536\t\n537\tTable~\\ref{tab:rsa-cka} reveals an important metric dissociation.\n538\tCKA yields an ordering reversal: untrained $>$ trained $>$ projector $>$ DINO, while RSA gives projector $>$ trained $\\approx$ untrained $>$ DINO.\n539\tDINO ranks lowest under both metrics---a robust finding.\n540\tThe dissociation implies that LLM training degrades local geometric alignment (RSA) while preserving or enhancing global structure (CKA).\n541\tThis ``geometric degradation + structural preservation'' is richer than either metric alone would suggest, and all geometric claims in this paper are explicitly qualified by metric.\n542\t\n543\t\\begin{table}[t]\n544\t\\centering\n545\t\\caption{RSA vs.\\ CKA ordering comparison. Ordering reversal between metrics reflects different aspects of representational structure.}\n546\t\\label{tab:rsa-cka}\n547\t\\small\n548\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n549\t\\toprule\n550\tSource & RSA Rank & Linear CKA Rank & RDM-CKA Rank \\\\\n551\t\\midrule\n552\tSigLIP+Proj  & 1st (0.615) & 3rd (0.081) & 2nd (0.257) \\\\\n553\tVLA Trained   & 2nd (0.488) & 4th (0.083) & 1st (0.260) \\\\\n554\tVLA Untrained & 3rd (0.485) & 2nd (0.089) & 3rd (0.246) \\\\\n555\tDINO          & 4th (0.388) & 5th (0.066) & 4th (0.241) \\\\\n556\t\\bottomrule\n557\t\\end{tabular}\n558\t\\end{table}\n559\t\n560\t\\subsubsection{Weight Analysis and Frozen Encoder Verification}\n561\t\\label{sec:weight-analysis}\n562\t\n563\tThe vision encoder and projector remain perfectly frozen during training (cosine similarity = 1.0 and 1.001, respectively).\n564\tLLM layers show uniform relative weight change (1.22--1.37$\\times$) across all 32 layers, with the output head at only 1.12$\\times$---ruling out shallow adaptation (threshold: 3$\\times$).\n565\tThis confirms that training-induced effects emerge from distributed LLM-wide reorganization, not localized head or layer modifications.\n566\t\n567\t%% ============================================================\n568\t%%  5. DISCUSSION\n569\t%% ============================================================\n570\t\\section{Discussion}\n571\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n572\t\n573\t\\paragraph{What VLA training does and does not do.}\n574\tOur cross-architecture analysis reveals a clean separation between training-induced effects and inherited properties.\n575\tVLA fine-tuning \\emph{does} create R$^2$ exit collapse and enhance counterfactual sensitivity---these are architecture-independent signatures observable across Llama-2-7B, Phi-3-mini, and Qwen2.5-VL-3B backbones.\n576\tVLA fine-tuning does \\emph{not} create L1 attention routing: this is inherited from the base model's pretraining (language modeling, vision-language alignment, or OXE pretraining), and task fine-tuning may even reduce the number of instruction-dominant heads.\n577\t\n578\t\\paragraph{The routing gradient.}\n579\tThe three-tier gradient---VLM base $\\to$ OXE VLA $\\to$ task VLA---suggests that instruction routing is progressively refined rather than created wholesale.\n580\tOXE pretraining establishes general-purpose routing (L1 heads, counterfactual sensitivity) without task specialization (no exit collapse).\n581\tTask fine-tuning then reshapes this routing, concentrating action-relevant information in mid-layers while allowing exit layers to specialize for action output.\n582\tThis distinction has practical implications: diagnostic tools can use exit collapse as a signature that task-specific routing has been learned.\n583\t\n584\t\\paragraph{Metric dependence of geometric findings.}\n585\tOur RSA and CKA results diverge on encoder rankings and LLM-layer trajectories.\n586\tRSA captures local geometric alignment (which LLM processing degrades), while CKA captures global structural preservation (which LLM processing maintains).\n587\tWe present this not as a limitation but as a richer characterization: VLA training simultaneously degrades fine-grained geometric alignment and preserves coarse structural organization.\n588\tAll geometric claims in this paper are explicitly qualified by the metric used.\n589\t\n590\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n591\tOur analysis is conducted in an open-loop setting without closed-loop robot evaluation, limiting claims about behavioral consequences.\n592\tThe LIBERO benchmark, while diverse, uses a fixed embodiment; generalization to other platforms remains untested.\n593\tNORA's 2-of-4 suite coverage (no Object/Long checkpoints) leaves gaps in the matrix.\n594\tThe OFT outlier (OpenVLA Long, $\\Delta$=0.004) suggests that parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods may circumvent the exit collapse signature, warranting further investigation.\n595\t\n596\t%% ============================================================\n597\t%%  6. CONCLUSION\n598\t%% ============================================================\n599\t\\section{Conclusion}\n600\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n601\t\n602\tWe presented a systematic token-level analysis framework for VLAs and applied it to three architectures across four task suites.\n603\tOur central finding is that VLA fine-tuning produces two architecture-independent training signatures---R$^2$ exit collapse and heightened counterfactual sensitivity---confirmed in 9 of 10 matrix cells across three VLA families.\n604\tA three-tier routing gradient reveals that instruction routing emerges at the VLM-to-VLA transition and reshapes with task specialization, with L1 attention routing inherited from the base model rather than created by fine-tuning.\n605\tThese findings provide mechanistic grounding for VLA design: entry-exit routing patterns and exit collapse diagnostics can guide architecture optimization and training monitoring.\n606\t\n607\t%% ============================================================\n608\t%%  REFERENCES\n609\t%% ============================================================\n610\t{\\small\n611\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n612\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n613\t\n614\t\\bibitem{belinkov2022probing}\n615\tY.~Belinkov.\n616\t\\newblock Probing classifiers: Promises, shortcomings, and advances.\n617\t\\newblock \\emph{Computational Linguistics}, 48(1):207--219, 2022.\n618\t\n619\t\\bibitem{brohan2023rt2}\n620\tA.~Brohan \\etal\n621\t\\newblock {RT-2}: Vision-language-action models transfer web knowledge to robotic control.\n622\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2023.\n623\t\n624\t\\bibitem{grant2026visual}\n625\tH.~Grant \\etal\n626\t\\newblock Visual dominance in vision-language-action models.\n627\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n628\t\n629\t\\bibitem{haon2025lost}\n630\tA.~Haon \\etal\n631\t\\newblock Lost in translation: Large language models in robotics instruction following.\n632\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICRA}, 2025.\n633\t\n634\t\\bibitem{kim2024openvla}\n635\tM.~Kim \\etal\n636\t\\newblock {OpenVLA}: An open-source vision-language-action model.\n637\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2024.\n638\t\n639\t\\bibitem{kornblith2019similarity}\n640\tS.~Kornblith, M.~Norouzi, H.~Lee, and G.~Hinton.\n641\t\\newblock Similarity of neural network representations revisited.\n642\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2019.\n643\t\n644\t\\bibitem{kriegeskorte2008representational}\n645\tN.~Kriegeskorte, M.~Mur, and P.~Bandettini.\n646\t\\newblock Representational similarity analysis.\n647\t\\newblock \\emph{Frontiers in Systems Neuroscience}, 2:4, 2008.\n648\t\n649\t\\end{thebibliography}\n650\t}\n651\t\n652\t\\end{document}\n653\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fkf1rVNGL1iMs2op5SNsMN","session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3","uuid":"23f6263f-2e6f-42c2-927c-75ec4d57fb1c","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:23:15.345Z"}
