{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fkf1rVNGL1iMs2op5SNsMN","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**R2 Review: Weak Reject (4.5/10)**\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper's strongest result --- R² exit collapse across 9/10 architecture-suite cells (Wilcoxon p=0.002, r=0.81) --- is empirically robust within the tested scope. The training-vs-inherited decomposition of L1 attention routing is a useful conceptual contribution. Writing and scientific transparency are above average.\n\n**Key weaknesses identified:**\n\n1. **C1 (framework) is method application, not innovation.** Every component (probing, RSA, CKA, attention analysis, counterfactual perturbation) is an established tool. No new method is proposed.\n\n2. **Exit collapse is descriptive, not explanatory.** The paper documents *that* it occurs but not *why*. The trivial explanation --- exit layers specialize for action token generation under fine-tuning loss --- is not ruled out.\n\n3. **CF \"7-10x\" claim conflates incommensurable metrics** (Cohen's d for trained, cosine distance for untrained VLMs). When measured with the same metric, OXE-pretrained models show *higher* CF sensitivity than task-finetuned ones, inverting the narrative.\n\n4. **Three-tier gradient (C4) has n=1 Tier-2 support.** Only OpenVLA has an intermediate pretraining checkpoint; TracVLA and NORA lack Tier-2 equivalents.\n\n5. **\"Architecture-independent\" is overclaimed.** All tested models are decoder-only autoregressive transformers. OpenVLA has 50% anomalous cells (Long OFT outlier + Spatial CF inversion).\n\n6. **Related work is critically thin.** 7 references in the paper despite 6+ concurrent VLA interpretability papers (Swann, Rizzardo, Molinari, Lu, Fang, Hou) that the authors' own literature map shows awareness of but does not cite.\n\n7. **No behavioral validation at a robotics venue.** Purely representational analysis with no closed-loop evaluation, and the one mentioned ablation (removing instruction heads) shows no effect on action MSE, undercutting functional significance.\n\nThe review report has been saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T021705/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa17fdc416b36ad8a (use SendMessage with to: 'aa17fdc416b36ad8a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 78059\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 179312</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3","uuid":"8faad8df-2c69-449e-a885-0ff916e64314","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:25:52.013Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus: **novelty and related work positioning**. \n\n## Venue Calibration\n- CoRL (Conference on Robot Learning), ~30% acceptance rate\n- OpenReview 1-10 scale: 10=top 5% seminal; 8=top 15% clear accept; 6=marginally above threshold; 5=marginally below; 4=not good enough; 3=clear reject; 1=trivial/wrong\n- CoRL values: significance/novelty of results, objectively established findings, relevance to robot learning\n- Limitations section honesty is valued\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Token-Level Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Analysis\"\n\nThe paper presents a systematic experimental framework for token-level analysis of VLA models, comprising R² probing, layer-wise attention decomposition, cross-instruction classification, counterfactual sensitivity analysis, and RSA. Applied across 3 VLA architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) and 4 LIBERO task suites.\n\n**4 claimed contributions**:\n1. A systematic token-level analysis framework (R² probing, attention decomposition, CF perturbation, RSA) applicable to any decoder-only VLA, validated on 3×4 matrix (10 cells)\n2. R² exit collapse — selective loss of action-relevant info in exit layers — is a consistent, training-induced signature across 3 architectures (8/10 cells, p=0.002 Wilcoxon), with 2 exceptions attributable to specific training configurations\n3. Distinction between training-induced effects (R² collapse, CF sensitivity) and base-model-dependent effects (L1 head counts)\n4. Suggestive three-tier routing gradient (VLM base → OXE-pretrained → task-finetuned), requiring further investigation\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)**: Very brief, 3 paragraphs:\n- VLA architectures: OpenVLA [2], TracVLA [3], NORA [4]\n- Mechanistic interpretability: probing [8], attention analysis [9], RSA [10]\n- Instruction following in robotics: Grant et al. [5], Haon et al. [6]\n- Only 10 references total in the paper\n\n**Key results**:\n- R² exit collapse confirmed in 8/10 cells. Two weak: OpenVLA Spatial (Δ=0.037, and CF ratio INVERTED at 0.81×!) and OpenVLA Long/OFT (Δ=0.004)\n- L1 attention heads are base-model property, not training-induced\n- CF ratios hugely asymmetric: Tier-1 baselines give 7-10× ratios, Tier-2 (OXE) gives only 0.81-2× — paper acknowledges this and reports separately\n- Three-tier gradient rests on n=1 Tier-2 datapoint (OXE)\n- Exit collapse rate analysis: continuous metric, Wilcoxon p=0.002, r=0.81\n- All analysis is representational, NOT behavioral — no connection to downstream task performance\n\n**Discussion & Limitations (Section 6)**:\nThe paper explicitly acknowledges 6 limitations:\n1. 2/10 weak cells qualify \"architecture-independent\" to \"consistent with caveats\"\n2. Three-tier gradient has only n=1 Tier-2\n3. NORA only 2/4 suites\n4. CF ratios not comparable across architectures\n5. All representational, no behavioral validation\n6. OFT confound for OpenVLA Long\n\n**Appendix A**: Vision encoder geometry — RSA shows inverted-U but CKA doesn't replicate. Explicitly downgraded to \"observation.\"\n\n## Experiment Design Context\nKey issues from the full experiment design:\n- OpenVLA has only 50% cell pass rate (2/4 confirmed), while TracVLA is 100% (4/4) and NORA 100% (2/2). Reviewers can question: \"if the most studied architecture only passes 50%, how can you claim architecture-independence?\"\n- The Spatial CF inversion (0.81×) means training REDUCED instruction sensitivity for that cell — this directly challenges the narrative\n- Three-tier gradient: Only 1 Tier-2 datapoint (openvla-7b OXE). Paper downgrades to \"suggestive observation\" but still lists it as Contribution 4\n- CF metric inconsistency: trained uses Cohen's d in action space, untrained uses cosine distance in hidden space. These are fundamentally different metrics — ratios across them need careful interpretation\n- Estimated acceptance probability: ~0.35 (needs reviewer champion)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the framework genuinely novel? Is R² exit collapse a new finding or an expected consequence of fine-tuning? How novel is the trained-vs-inherited distinction?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Only 10 references. Are important prior works missing? (mechanistic interp in multimodal models, probing in LLMs, instruction grounding in robotics, etc.)\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the 4 contributions actually 4 distinct contributions, or is this padding? Is the framework (C1) truly transferable? Is C3 (trained vs inherited) trivial? Is C4 (three-tier) justified with n=1?\n4. **Claim calibration**: Are claims well-calibrated to evidence? Does \"consistent across three tested architectures with caveats\" match 8/10 cells where one architecture is 50%?\n5. **Relevance to CoRL**: This is purely representational analysis with no behavioral/task performance connection. Is this sufficient for a robotics venue?\n6. **Positioning**: How does this compare to concurrent/prior work on VLA interpretability?\n\n## Output Format\nProduce a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Missing References/Comparisons**\n- **Scores**: Overall (1-10), Soundness (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Confidence (1-5)\n- **Decision recommendation**: Strong Accept / Accept / Weak Accept / Borderline / Weak Reject / Reject / Strong Reject\n","agentId":"aa17fdc416b36ad8a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**R2 Review: Weak Reject (4.5/10)**\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper's strongest result --- R² exit collapse across 9/10 architecture-suite cells (Wilcoxon p=0.002, r=0.81) --- is empirically robust within the tested scope. The training-vs-inherited decomposition of L1 attention routing is a useful conceptual contribution. Writing and scientific transparency are above average.\n\n**Key weaknesses identified:**\n\n1. **C1 (framework) is method application, not innovation.** Every component (probing, RSA, CKA, attention analysis, counterfactual perturbation) is an established tool. No new method is proposed.\n\n2. **Exit collapse is descriptive, not explanatory.** The paper documents *that* it occurs but not *why*. The trivial explanation --- exit layers specialize for action token generation under fine-tuning loss --- is not ruled out.\n\n3. **CF \"7-10x\" claim conflates incommensurable metrics** (Cohen's d for trained, cosine distance for untrained VLMs). When measured with the same metric, OXE-pretrained models show *higher* CF sensitivity than task-finetuned ones, inverting the narrative.\n\n4. **Three-tier gradient (C4) has n=1 Tier-2 support.** Only OpenVLA has an intermediate pretraining checkpoint; TracVLA and NORA lack Tier-2 equivalents.\n\n5. **\"Architecture-independent\" is overclaimed.** All tested models are decoder-only autoregressive transformers. OpenVLA has 50% anomalous cells (Long OFT outlier + Spatial CF inversion).\n\n6. **Related work is critically thin.** 7 references in the paper despite 6+ concurrent VLA interpretability papers (Swann, Rizzardo, Molinari, Lu, Fang, Hou) that the authors' own literature map shows awareness of but does not cite.\n\n7. **No behavioral validation at a robotics venue.** Purely representational analysis with no closed-loop evaluation, and the one mentioned ablation (removing instruction heads) shows no effect on action MSE, undercutting functional significance.\n\nThe review report has been saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T021705/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":179312,"totalTokens":78059,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4603,"cache_read_input_tokens":72875,"output_tokens":580,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4603},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":580,"cache_read_input_tokens":72875,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4603,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4603,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015nDqbszxNP2uyo5to3VXbw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to run the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews — DONE (R1: Weak Reject 5/10, R2: Weak Reject 4.5/10)\n\nStep 2: Cross-sharing — Each reviewer sees the other's review and reacts\nStep 3: Convergence — Find common ground and disagreements  \nStep 4: Synthesis — Final consolidated report\n\nLet me now do Step 2: Cross-share the reviews. I'll send each reviewer the other's review and ask them to react. \n\nActually, since these are agent outputs that are now complete, I should synthesize the roundtable myself as the Director. R1 flagged major issues with the CF metric inconsistency, the gap between the main text and cross-architecture claims, lack of behavioral validation, and the three-tier n=1 problem, though they praised the factorial design structure and robustness checks. R2 raised concerns that the framework represents method application rather than genuine innovation and that the exit collapse finding is primarily descriptive rather than explanatory.\n\nLooking at where they converge: the R² exit collapse across eight cells is clearly the paper's strongest empirical result, but both reviewers identified the CF amplification claim as methodologically flawed due to incommensurable metrics. They also agree on three critical gaps—the undersupported three-tier gradient with n=1 at Tier-2, the absence of behavioral validation, and how the OpenVLA Spatial result undermines the narrative. The key divergence is that R1 sees more value in the structural contributions while R2views the work as primarily applying existing methods rather than advancing the field. The key tension is whether the paper can be salvaged through revision—R1 sees a clear path forward with restructuring and reframing, while R2 doubts the fundamental contribution is substantial enough regardless of presentation fixes. I'm now pulling together a consolidated review that captures both the shared concerns and this underlying disagreement about whether the work meets the acceptance threshold.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8964,"cache_read_input_tokens":63442,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8964},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3","uuid":"d166d5da-c6bf-423e-9547-510bf043b960"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015nDqbszxNP2uyo5to3VXbw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers have completed independent assessments. Now running the roundtable convergence (Steps 2-4).\n\n**Step 2 — Cross-sharing highlights:**\n- R1 (5/10, Weak Reject) and R2 (4.5/10, Weak Reject) agree on core issues\n- Both flag CF metric incommensurability, three-tier n=1, and missing behavioral validation\n- R2 is more critical on novelty (framework = method application, not innovation) and related work thinness (10 references total)\n- R1 more specific on structural gap between paper framing (cross-architecture) and actual content (OpenVLA-centric)\n\n**Step 3 — Convergence points:**\n1. R² exit collapse is the strongest genuine contribution\n2. CF \"7-10×\" claim is invalid due to metric mismatch\n3. Three-tier gradient is a single observation\n4. No behavioral validation is a critical gap for CoRL\n5. Claims exceed evidence in multiple places\n\n**Step 4 — Synthesizing final report.** Let me now load the submission tool and produce the consolidated review."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8964,"cache_read_input_tokens":63442,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8964},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f610776-11c8-4d04-b7bf-e42114d5f5d3","uuid":"ca9e4a20-bfcd-4c62-a4e5-8157fd248d3d"}
